<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Hybrid Architectures</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Preliminaries: Attention and State Space Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An auto-regressive language model is a stack of <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decoder layers</em>, each composed of a <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sequence-mixing</em> sublayer followed by a feed-forward network (also referred to as a Multilayer Perceptron or MLP).
The sequence-mixing sublayer is the component that aggregates information across token positions, the two families of sequence-mixing layers relevant to this work, Attention and State Space Models, differ fundamentally in <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> they store and access past context.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Attention.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an input sequence of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> token representations <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=\{\textbf{x}_{1},\textbf{x}_{2},\dots,\textbf{x}_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=\{\textbf{x}_{1},\textbf{x}_{2},\dots,\textbf{x}_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, a single causal Attention head computes query, key, and value vectors <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{q}_{t},\bm{k}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{q}_{t},\bm{k}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> at each position <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> via learned non-linear functions of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              For example, Qwen3 employs linear projection followed by unit normalization of the query and key vectors.</span></span></span>. The output at position <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is then</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{t}=\sum_{i=1}^{t}\frac{\exp(\bm{q}_{t}^{\top}\bm{k}_{i})}{\sum_{j=1}^{t}\exp(\bm{q}_{t}^{\top}\bm{k}_{j})}\;\bm{v}_{i}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{t}=\sum_{i=1}^{t}\frac{\exp(\bm{q}_{t}^{\top}\bm{k}_{i})}{\sum_{j=1}^{t}\exp(\bm{q}_{t}^{\top}\bm{k}_{j})}\;\bm{v}_{i}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">a softmax-weighted retrieval over all past key–value pairs.
At inference time, the pairs <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{k}_{i},\bm{v}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{k}_{i},\bm{v}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,t</annotation></semantics></math> are stored verbatim in a <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">KV cache</em> that grows
linearly with context length. This cache is an <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">eidetic memory</em>: every token representation is retained exactly, providing precise access to the full context at a memory and compute cost that scales with the number of tokens stored.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">State Space Models (SSMs).</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An SSM replaces the growing KV cache with a fixed-size <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">recurrent state</em> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}\times d_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}\times d_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> that is updated at each time step and read out to produce the layer’s output <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}+\bm{v}_{t}\,\bm{B}_{t},\qquad\bm{y}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t}\,\bm{q}_{t}\ +\bm{v}_{t}\,D_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}+\bm{v}_{t}\,\bm{B}_{t},\qquad\bm{y}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t}\,\bm{q}_{t}\ +\bm{v}_{t}\,D_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}\times d_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}\times d_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">transition operator</em> that controls how the previous state is transformed, and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gating operator</em> that controls how the incoming how the incoming key-value pair is written into the state.
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">feedthrough</em> scalar that controls how much of the current input is passed directly to the output without being mediated by the state. The state <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as a <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fading memory</em>: it compresses the entire history into a representation of bounded size, yielding <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> memory regardless of context length, but inherently losing information as the sequence grows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Different SSM families are distinguished by their choice of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>; we describe the three families used in this work in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 SSM Layer Types ‣ 4 SSM Layers for Fading Memory in Hybrids ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. Note that when <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}=\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}=\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math> (the identity) and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}_{t}=\bm{k}_{t}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}_{t}=\bm{k}_{t}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, the recurrence reduces to vanilla Linear Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Katharopoulos et al. (2020)</a></cite>, making the SSM framework a strict generalisation of Linear Attention.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Hybrid Models and Priming</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hybrid model</em>, in our usage, is an auto-regressive language model that employs <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">both</em> Attention layers and SSM layers as sequence-mixing sublayers within the same network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zancato et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Standard Transformers store all tokens verbatim in a KV cache that grows linearly with context, making long-context training expensive and inference demanding in both memory and latency. Hybrid models address this by replacing a subset of Attention layers with recurrent SSM layers, each of which maintains a fixed-dimensional latent state that acts as a compressed summary of the remote past. Because only the remaining Attention layers require a KV cache, the overall cache footprint shrinks and decoding becomes faster. The resulting architecture balances two complementary memory regimes: eidetic memory from Attention (quadratic cost) and fading memory from SSMs (linear cost) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zancato et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Several recent efforts have developed Hybrid models trained from scratch. Jamba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lieber et al. (2024)</a></cite>, Griffin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">De et al. (2024)</a></cite>, Samba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al. (2025)</a></cite>, the Nemotron family <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blakeman et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Basant et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blakeman et al. (2025b)</a></cite> and B’MOJO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zancato et al. (2024)</a></cite> each propose architectures that interleave Attention and SSM layers in a regular pattern or that combine Attention and SSMs within the same layer like in B’MOJO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zancato et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
These approaches demonstrate strong results but require the full cost of pre-training from a random initialization.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Priming.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work takes a different approach.
<em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Priming</em> instantiates a Hybrid model from the weights of an existing pre-trained Transformer, using both its architecture and its learned parameters as a starting point.
This avoids the cost of training from scratch and enables any Transformer with public weights to serve as a <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">source model</span>.
For context, the pre-training of Qwen3-8B consumed 36 trillion tokens; our Primed Hybrid 8B models require fewer than 150 billion training tokens, less than <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the pre-training budget.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Priming Design Space</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Three axes parameterize the space of Primed Hybrid architectures:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hybrid ratio</span>: the fraction of sequence-mixing
layers that are SSM layers. A 75% ratio means three out of every four sequence-mixing layers are SSMs; higher ratios yield greater inference efficiency at the cost of reduced eidetic capacity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">layer pattern</span>: the specific assignment of each position in the layer stack to either Attention or SSM. As Priming instantiates a Hybrid model from the weights of a pre-trained Transformer, the choice of <em id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which</em> layers are SSM layers matters: not all layers in the source Transformer are equally amenable to SSM instantiation. (<a href="#S3.SS1.SSS3" title="3.1.3 From Theory to Practice ‣ 3.1 Realization-theoretic Foundations for Attention-to-SSM Priming ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.3</span></a>). We address this via importance-based layer selection in <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" title="Importance-based layer selection algorithm. ‣ 3.2.1 Layer Selection: Avoiding the Hardest Cases ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SSM layer type</span>: which SSM family is used for the
fading-memory layers. We evaluate three representatives: Mamba-2, Gated DeltaNet (GDN), and Gated KalmaNet (GKA). Each can optionally be paired with Sliding Window Attention within a single Hybrid sublayer (the B’MOJO-F configuration described in <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Hybrid Layers: B’MOJO-F ‣ 4 SSM Layers for Fading Memory in Hybrids ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>The Fading–Eidetic Tradeoff</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The design of a Hybrid model is fundamentally a question of how to balance two kinds of memory.
An Attention layer stores every key–value pair <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{k}_{i},\bm{v}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{k}_{i},\bm{v}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> exactly in its KV cache, providing precise retrieval at a cost that grows with context length. An SSM layer compresses the entire history into a single state matrix <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}\times d_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}\times d_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, bounding memory regardless of context length but inevitably losing information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a target Hybrid architecture with a desired ratio of eidetic to fading memory layers (e.g. 50%), the key design question is which layers should have eidetic and which should have fading memory. We use the source Transformer to answer this: its layer-wise behavior reveals where eidetic memory is structurally necessary and where fading memory suffices, guiding the placement of each layer type in the Hybrid. Identifying this assignment is the subject of our layer selection procedure (<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" title="Importance-based layer selection algorithm. ‣ 3.2.1 Layer Selection: Avoiding the Hardest Cases ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>), and the formal conditions under which a fading-memory SSM can approximate an Attention layer’s function are developed next in <a href="#S3" title="3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>What Is Priming and Why Does It Work?</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training a Hybrid LLM from scratch demands the full cost of pre-training. <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Priming</em> sidesteps this by initializing a Hybrid model directly from a pre-trained Transformer’s weights, a cross-architecture knowledge transfer that consumes fewer than <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the source Transformer’s pre-training token budget. <em id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Why should such a transfer work at all?</em>
Attention and SSM layers compute different input-output mappings: one produces a softmax-weighted retrieval over an eidetic cache, the other evolves a compressed recurrent state through learned transitions.
On what grounds can an SSM inherit the representations induced by Attention weights? In this section we develop a principled answer, grounded in the key ideas of classical realization theory of dynamical systems.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Realization-theoretic Foundations for Attention-to-SSM Priming</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">When and why can an SSM layer recover the representations of an Attention layer in a pre-trained Transformer?</em>
Classical realization theory from system identification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lindquist and Picci, 1979</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patrick Dewilde, 1998</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ljung, 1999</a>)</cite> gives us a useful language to design and understand our Priming pipeline.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Attention as an Input-Output Map</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a single Attention head in a layer of the source Transformer. Given an input sequence <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, causal (auto-regressive) self-attention computes</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{X})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{X}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the matrix of value projections and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mixing matrix</em>, lower-triangular (causal), row-stochastic (due to softmax), and input-dependent (function of keys and queries). The entry <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes how much token <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> contributes to the representation of token <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following the <em id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">matrix mixer</em> abstraction introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao and Gu (2024)</a></cite> and operationalized for distillation by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bick et al. (2024)</a></cite>, both Attention and SSMs can be viewed as applying a <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times T</annotation></semantics></math> mixing matrix to a token sequence. Attention produces <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> via softmax over query-key inner products while an SSM with state dimension <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> produces a lower-triangular semi-separable matrix <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lindquist and Picci (1979)</a></cite>, determined by the inputs and the recurrent dynamics. This shared abstraction is the foundation for Priming: if the SSM’s mixing matrix can represent the Transformer’s, that is, if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{X})\approx\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{X})\approx\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, then the SSM inherits the Attention layer’s input-output behavior and the value projections and downstream MLPs can be transferred directly. This mixing matrix <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore the central object of interest for Priming Hybrid architectures. In the next section we develop theoretical connections that characterize when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> can be realized by an SSM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>The Realization Theory Problem</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An SSM with state dimension <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., Mamba-2, GDN, GKA) yields a <em id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finite-horizon input-output matrix</em><span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              We refer to the SSM’s mixing matrix as its <em id="footnote5.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finite-horizon input-output matrix</em> to distinguish it from Attention’s mixing matrix <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> that relates inputs to outputs over a length-<math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> sequence (see <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Mixing Matrix and Finite-Horizon Input-Output Matrix ‣ Appendix B Realization Theory ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> for the explicit form for a general linear time-varying SSM). This matrix is lower-triangular and <em id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semi-separable of order <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></em>: every submatrix lying strictly below the main diagonal has rank at most <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, because all information from the past must pass through the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional state bottleneck.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Classical realization theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patrick Dewilde, 1998</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lindquist and Picci, 1979</a>)</cite> studies the following question: given a causal input-output map, here represented by the lower-triangular Attention mixing matrix <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, what is the smallest state dimension <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> of a state-space model that can realize it exactly?</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The key tool used in the literature of linear input-varying SSMs is the rank of the <em id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hankel submatrices</em>, which measure how much information must be carried from the past into the future. A Hankel submatrix is obtained by cutting the mixing matrix at time <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and retaining only the block that maps past inputs to future outputs. Its rank tells us how many degrees of freedom must pass through the latent state.
For each split point <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, define <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{H}(\mathbf{X})_{k}=\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})_{k+1:T,1:k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}(\mathbf{X})_{k}=\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})_{k+1:T,1:k},</annotation></semantics></math> the block that maps inputs from the first <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> timesteps (the “past”) to outputs from timesteps <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1,\dots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1,\dots,T</annotation></semantics></math> (the “future”). Intuitively, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}(\mathbf{X})_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}(\mathbf{X})_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the past-to-future influence across the time cut at instant <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
The minimal state dimension to realize the input-output causal map (given by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>)) is then</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\min}(\mathbf{X})=\max_{1\leq k&lt;T}\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{H}(\mathbf{X})_{k})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\min}(\mathbf{X})=\max_{1\leq k&lt;T}\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{H}(\mathbf{X})_{k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(Hankel rank)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">At one extreme, any <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times T</annotation></semantics></math> lower-triangular matrix can always be realized by an SSM with state dimension <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, by storing the entire history in the state. This is a trivial bound that defeats the purpose of using a compact recurrent state. The key result from realization theory is that there exists an SSM with state <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\ll T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\ll T</annotation></semantics></math> that can realize <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> exactly whenever the Hankel rank is bounded by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. We make this notion precise in the following theorem that adapts the classical time-varying realization theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patrick Dewilde, 1998</a>, Theorem 3.7)</cite> to the causal Attention setting of our work, we refer the reader to our proof in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Realization Theory ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. <span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              The actual SSM layers in this work operate on matrix-valued states
<math id="footnote6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}\times d_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}\times d_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> with vector-valued inputs and
outputs. We show how to adapt the classical proof for the scalar input-output case in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Realization Theory ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. The key insight is that the <em id="footnote6.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finite-horizon input-output matrix</em> acts identically and independently on each of the <math id="footnote6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> columns, so the analysis applies element-wise.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Finite-Horizon Per-Instance Realization of the Attention Map)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a sequence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the sequence of values, and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> be the causal Attention mixing matrix of a single head.
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\leq t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\leq t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input sequence up to time <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a linear time-varying state-space realization</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})+\bm{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{B}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t}),\qquad\bm{y}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})+\bm{v}_{t}\,D_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})+\bm{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{B}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t}),\qquad\bm{y}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})+\bm{v}_{t}\,D_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>, zero initial state, and state dimension <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\min}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\min}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, whose finite-horizon input-output matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly the mixing matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, no realization of this finite-horizon input-output map with smaller state dimension exists.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Finite-Horizon Per-Instance Realization of the Attention Map). ‣ 3.1.2 The Realization Theory Problem ‣ 3.1 Realization-theoretic Foundations for Attention-to-SSM Priming ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> is encouraging but presents two practical challenges for initializing Hybrid models from a pre-trained
Transformer via Priming. <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(i) Per-instance existence.</span> The guarantee
holds for a fixed <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>: different input sequences generally induce
different minimal realizations, with different state dimensions and different
parameter maps <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{B}_{t},\mathbf{C}_{t},D_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{B}_{t},\mathbf{C}_{t},D_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. An SSM layer in a Primed Hybrid model, however, is expected to operate across multiple input sequences with a single shared parametrization. <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(ii) Arbitrarily large Hankel rank.</span> Since Attention is eidetic,
nothing constrains its mixing matrix to be low-rank across time cuts, that is,
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\min}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\min}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> can be as large as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Approximating <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> with an SSM of state size <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\ll T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\ll T</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore prone to approximation errors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Priming addresses these challenges on two fronts. For (i), rather than realizing a per-instance SSM, we optimize a <em id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single</em> SSM parametrization
over the training distribution, so the realization is amortized across inputs rather than constructed per-sequence. For (ii), we exploit the empirical
observation that <a href="#S3.Ex1" title="Equation Hankel rank ‣ 3.1.2 The Realization Theory Problem ‣ 3.1 Realization-theoretic Foundations for Attention-to-SSM Priming ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Hankel rank</span></a> varies substantially across layers: some layers perform local computations that an SSM can
capture with modest state size, while others perform complex, long-range,
input-dependent retrieval that requires a large state dimension to realize with an SSM. Our Hybrid architecture is therefore designed around this heterogeneity: high-Hankel-rank
layers are kept as Attention, and only layers amenable to low-rank realization are replaced with SSMs. <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" title="Importance-based layer selection algorithm. ‣ 3.2.1 Layer Selection: Avoiding the Hardest Cases ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a> describes our selection
procedure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.3 </span>From Theory to Practice</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The difficulty of transferring Attention layers into SSM layers is that, <em id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a priori</em>, we do not know how the <a href="#S3.Ex1" title="Equation Hankel rank ‣ 3.1.2 The Realization Theory Problem ‣ 3.1 Realization-theoretic Foundations for Attention-to-SSM Priming ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Hankel rank</span></a> of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> varies across the input distribution. The learned SSM parameterization must therefore handle the full distribution, including the high-rank Attention patterns that strain the SSM’s capacity. This is what makes naive one-shot distillation insufficient and motivates the multi-stage design of Priming:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Avoid the hardest cases.</em> Layer selection (<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" title="Importance-based layer selection algorithm. ‣ 3.2.1 Layer Selection: Avoiding the Hardest Cases ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>) identifies Attention layers in the source Transformer whose mixing matrices are empirically most complex (high <a href="#S3.Ex1" title="Equation Hankel rank ‣ 3.1.2 The Realization Theory Problem ‣ 3.1 Realization-theoretic Foundations for Attention-to-SSM Priming ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Hankel rank</span></a>) and keeps them as Attention in our Hybrid model. Thus, rather than forcing an approximation that the theory predicts will be poor, or that requires a prohibitively large state size <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we keep those layers as Attention.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Start from a good initialization.</em> Following layer selection, Stage 0 (<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS2" title="3.2.2 Stage 0: Transferring Knowledge from Source Transformer ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.2</span></a>) uses the Attention-SSM correspondence to initialize the SSM layers in our Hybrid model near a known (yet imperfect) realization, rather than from random weights.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Provide enough capacity to cover the input distribution.</em> State expansion (<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS3" title="3.2.3 State Expansion via Adaptive GQA ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.3</span></a>) increases the SSM’s degrees of freedom, through learned projections, so that the parameterization has more room to represent complex temporal patterns.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Align per-layer structure.</em> Stage 1 (<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS4" title="3.2.4 Stage 1: Layerwise Alignment ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.4</span></a>) trains the SSM layers in the Hybrid model to match the Attention layer’s actual output (in the source Transformer) across a distribution of inputs, this is the practical <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">amortized</span> version of the per-instance realization algorithm.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Align to post-training data distribution.</em> Stage 2 (<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS5" title="3.2.5 Stage 2: Task Adaptation ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.5</span></a>) optimizes the full Hybrid model with cross-entropy loss end-to-end to obtain strong models on downstream domains of interest for the users (e.g. long-context reasoning and instruction following).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The next sections describe the key ideas in each stage of our Priming pipeline in detail.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In principle, one could compute the <a href="#S3.Ex1" title="Equation Hankel rank ‣ 3.1.2 The Realization Theory Problem ‣ 3.1 Realization-theoretic Foundations for Attention-to-SSM Priming ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Hankel rank</span></a> of each Attention layer’s mixing matrix per input and amortize these realizations into a single SSM parameterization. However, this is not practical since, the entire input distribution cannot be enumerated at training time, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is never materialized on the GPU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al. (2022)</a></cite>, and no closed-form amortization of the per-input realizations into a single SSM parameterization exists. Priming addresses each of these obstacles through a sequence of pragmatic approximations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Layer Selection: Avoiding the Hardest Cases</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Realization theory predicts that Attention layers with high <a href="#S3.Ex1" title="Equation Hankel rank ‣ 3.1.2 The Realization Theory Problem ‣ 3.1 Realization-theoretic Foundations for Attention-to-SSM Priming ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Hankel rank</span></a> are the hardest to approximate with a fixed-size SSM, so we keep those layers as Attention. Computing the <a href="#S3.Ex1" title="Equation Hankel rank ‣ 3.1.2 The Realization Theory Problem ‣ 3.1 Realization-theoretic Foundations for Attention-to-SSM Priming ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Hankel rank</span></a> directly is impractical, so we use a cheap empirical proxy: replace each Attention layer individually with Sliding Window Attention (SWA) of window <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and measure the resulting degradation on a set of target benchmarks. A SWA mixer has banded structure, which caps its Hankel rank at <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly across inputs; a layer that tolerates this substitution therefore has low effective Hankel rank and is a natural candidate for SSM instantiation. Conversely, layers that degrade sharply under SWA rely on high-rank structure (long-range retrieval, global aggregation, sharp input-dependent routing) that a fixed-size SSM would struggle to realize, and we retain these as Attention in the Primed Hybrid. We refer to the per-layer SWA-induced degradation as the layer’s <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">importance</em>; layers with high importance are those most sensitive to the SWA substitution and hence predicted by the theory to have high Hankel rank.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Empirically, this proxy works well. Importance-based layer selection consistently outperforms uniform (evenly spaced) placement across all model scales we tested (<a href="#S5.SS3.SSS1" title="5.3.1 Layer Selection ‣ 5.3 Priming Pipeline Ablation ‣ 5 Priming: Main Results ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3.1</span></a>). Alternative heuristics, such as concentrating Attention layers in the early or late portions of the network, degrade quality; in our experiments the high-importance layers do not follow a regular or predictable pattern across the source Transformer (see <a href="#S5.F4" title="In Importance Score Analysis. ‣ 5.3.1 Layer Selection ‣ 5.3 Priming Pipeline Ablation ‣ 5 Priming: Main Results ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Importance-based layer selection algorithm.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each Attention layer <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in the source Transformer, we create a variant in which only layer <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced with SWA of window size <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                We use <math id="footnote7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=2048" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=2048</annotation></semantics></math> in all our experiments.</span></span></span> and evaluate this variant on a set of tasks representative of long-context abilities, such as many-shot in-context learning, retrieval-augmented generation, and passage re-ranking. The <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">importance</em> of layer <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is the performance drop relative to the unmodified baseline. Given a target of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> SSM layers (determined by the desired Hybrid ratio), we rank all layers by their importance scores and instantiate the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> least important layers as SSM layers. The full procedure is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix C Layer Selection Procedure ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The resulting selection pattern defines the Hybrid architecture produced by Stage 0 of Priming (<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS2" title="3.2.2 Stage 0: Transferring Knowledge from Source Transformer ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S5.SS3.SSS1" title="5.3.1 Layer Selection ‣ 5.3 Priming Pipeline Ablation ‣ 5 Priming: Main Results ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3.1</span></a>, we apply this procedure to Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-32B and find that importance is concentrated in a small set of middle-to-late layers (Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Importance Score Analysis. ‣ 5.3.1 Layer Selection ‣ 5.3 Priming Pipeline Ablation ‣ 5 Priming: Main Results ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Using the resulting selection pattern during Priming outperforms a uniform (evenly spaced) pattern by 30% (relative) on long-context aggregate performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Stage 0: Transferring Knowledge from Source Transformer</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Stage 0 provides a good initialization for the SSM layers by exploiting a structural correspondence between Attention and the SSM recurrence. The correspondence motivates a direct transfer of the source Transformer’s projection matrices into the Hybrid’s SSM layers, without training. This lets Priming bootstrap the alignment between Hybrid SSM layers and their source Attention counterparts by re-using the Transformer’s pre-trained weights rather than starting from random initialization.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Query, key, and value projections.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As described in <a href="#S2" title="2 Hybrid Architectures ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, both Attention and SSM layers perform their computations on a common set of query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, key <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and value <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> vectors (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Attention. ‣ 2.1 Preliminaries: Attention and State Space Models ‣ 2 Hybrid Architectures ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S2.E2" title="In State Space Models (SSMs). ‣ 2.1 Preliminaries: Attention and State Space Models ‣ 2 Hybrid Architectures ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), obtained by linear projections <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{Q},\mathbf{W}_{K},\mathbf{W}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{Q},\mathbf{W}_{K},\mathbf{W}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> applied to the input <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Priming exploits this correspondence by initializing the SSM’s projection matrices directly from the corresponding Attention layer in the source Transformer.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Output and gating projections.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond these parameters, both Attention and SSM layers employ an output projection <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{O}</annotation></semantics></math> that maps per-head outputs back to the model dimension, which we transfer directly from the source Attention layer. The SSM additionally has a gating projection <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> that modulates the recurrent readout <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> before it is mapped by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{O}</annotation></semantics></math>. Although <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> has no Attention counterpart, it operates in the same representational space as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{O}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we initialize it from these two matrices (details and ablation in <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Gate Initialization ‣ Appendix A Priming Implementation Details ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>) to reuse the knowledge stored in the source Transformer’s weights rather than starting from random initialization as in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. The remaining SSM-specific parameters (transition dynamics, convolutions, and other layer-type-specific components) have no Attention counterpart and are initialized randomly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Other Hybrid components.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All remaining components of the Hybrid model, the MLPs, layer norms, embeddings, LM head, and the Attention layers retained by layer selection (<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" title="Importance-based layer selection algorithm. ‣ 3.2.1 Layer Selection: Avoiding the Hardest Cases ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>), are copied directly from the source Transformer. These components are unchanged by the Hybrid architecture, so their pre-trained weights transfer without any modification.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This initialization is not the exact parameter setting for the SSM that realizes the Attention layer’s mixing matrix: while realization theory guarantees that such a setting exists, copying the Attention projections gives us an approximate surrogate rather than the true solution. The transferred projections already encode the subspaces in which the Attention layer operates, giving the SSM a well-aligned starting point. Stage 1 (<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS4" title="3.2.4 Stage 1: Layerwise Alignment ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.4</span></a>) closes the remaining gap by learning parameters that amortize the realization across the input data distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>State Expansion via Adaptive GQA</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Realization theory tells us that the SSM state must carry enough degrees of freedom to approximate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> across the input distribution (see <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS2" title="3.1.2 The Realization Theory Problem ‣ 3.1 Realization-theoretic Foundations for Attention-to-SSM Priming ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.2</span></a>). A grouped KV structure (inherited from the source Transformer during Stage 0) reduces the SSM’s effective state capacity and thus its ability to meet this requirement. We unpack this mismatch below, then describe our State Expansion approach as a remedy to restore the SSM’s capacity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Modern Transformers use Grouped Query Attention (GQA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ainslie et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, where a small number of key-value heads <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathrm{KV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>KV</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathrm{KV}}</annotation></semantics></math> are shared across a larger number of query heads <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since Stage 0 reuses the Transformer’s projection weights to initialize the SSM layers, this grouped structure carries over to the Hybrid model. In Attention, sharing KV heads does not substantially degrade performance, since the full token history is retained in the reduced KV cache.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The SSM state, by contrast, is already a compressed summary of the entire history. As defined in <a href="#S2.E2" title="In State Space Models (SSMs). ‣ 2.1 Preliminaries: Attention and State Space Models ‣ 2 Hybrid Architectures ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the transition operator <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the gating operator <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are functions of the current key <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{k}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{k}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, with value <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> being written to the state through <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}_{t}\,\bm{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}_{t}\,\bm{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S4.T1" title="In Expressiveness hierarchy. ‣ 4.1 SSM Layer Types ‣ 4 SSM Layers for Fading Memory in Hybrids ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for the specific forms in each SSM we consider). When <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{k}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{k}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are shared across the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=H_{Q}/H_{\mathrm{KV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>KV</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=H_{Q}/H_{\mathrm{KV}}</annotation></semantics></math> query heads in a group, the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{k}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{k}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>-dependent components of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are shared too, and the state updates for heads in the same group can differ only through per-head gating/decay parameters (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t},\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t},\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, etc.). This reduces the SSM’s effective state capacity relative to having <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> independent key-value heads. The per-head query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{q}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{q}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> controls only what is read from the resulting state, and cannot recover information that was never written or was already forgotten. Attention does not suffer from this restriction: the full history is retained verbatim in the KV cache (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Attention. ‣ 2.1 Preliminaries: Attention and State Space Models ‣ 2 Hybrid Architectures ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), and each query head can retrospectively select different information via softmax-weighted routing, even when keys and values are shared within a group.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adaptive GQA (AGQA).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To recover per-head diversity without abandoning the GQA initialization, we introduce Adaptive GQA (AGQA). Instead of replicating each KV head <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> times identically, we learn a lightweight, low-rank projection that maps the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathrm{KV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>KV</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathrm{KV}}</annotation></semantics></math> shared heads to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> potentially distinct ones. A residual connection ensures the projection starts as standard GQA replication (by initializing the learned branch to zero) and gradually deviates during training, introducing per-head variation only as needed (see <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Adaptive GQA (AGQA) ‣ Appendix A Priming Implementation Details ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> for details).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">AGQA adds less than <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the total model parameters for Primed Hybrid 32B models. A related method, M1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, uses a full linear projection requiring <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\sim}1.8\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><mn>1.8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}1.8\times</annotation></semantics></math> more parameters than AGQA and no residual connection, so its projection must learn the GQA mapping from scratch. By recovering per-head diversity with minimal overhead, AGQA allows the SSM layers of our Primed Hybrid model to better utilize their state capacity despite inheriting a grouped KV structure from the source Transformer.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.4 </span>Stage 1: Layerwise Alignment</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Stage 0 initializes the SSM via a correspondence with the Attention layer’s projection matrices. However, this yields only an approximate realization of the Attention layer. Stage 1 closes this gap by <em id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">amortizing</em> the per-instance realization argument across the data distribution: we train the SSM parameters on generic web text data (<a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Stage 1: Alignment ‣ 10 Training Recipes ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>). Concretely, we use the source Transformer as a teacher and train the SSM layers of the Hybrid to replicate the teacher’s outputs. This is a <span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">practical proxy</span> for aligning the SSM’s finite-horizon input-output matrix <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> with the Attention mixing matrix <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, without having to materialize the full <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\times T</annotation></semantics></math> matrices. Two alignment objectives naturally arise.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Layerwise Alignment Objectives.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define a <em id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decoder layer</em> as a block consisting of a sequence mixer (Attention or SSM) and an MLP. Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> denote the total number of decoder layers, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th decoder layer, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{i}=F_{i}(\mathbf{X}_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{i}=F_{i}(\mathbf{X}_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math> the hidden states after layer <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the sequence of input token embeddings. The alignment objectives operate on hidden states via a Mean-Squared Error (MSE) loss. The following are the MSE objectives considered in this work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"> </p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">End-to-end MSE.</em> The loss is applied once, on the final hidden states before the LM head:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{e2e}}=\bigl\|\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{Hybrid}}-\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{Transformer}}\bigr\|^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>e2e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>Hybrid</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>Transformer</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{e2e}}=\bigl\|\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{Hybrid}}-\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{Transformer}}\bigr\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{Hybrid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>Hybrid</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{Hybrid}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{Transformer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>Transformer</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{Transformer}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the final-layer hidden states of the Hybrid model and the source Transformer respectively, each passed through the final layer norm (pre-LM Head representation) before comparison. This objective gives the SSM layers freedom to adjust their own internal representations, they need only match the teacher at the final output, not at every intermediate layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"> </p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Layerwise MSE.</em> The loss is applied at each decoder layer independently:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{LW}}=\frac{1}{\ell}\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}\bigl\|F_{i}^{\mathrm{Transformer}}(\mathbf{X}_{i-1}^{\mathrm{Transformer}})-F_{i}^{\mathrm{Hybrid}}(\mathbf{X}_{i-1}^{\mathrm{Hybrid}})\bigr\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>LW</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Transformer</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Transformer</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Hybrid</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>Hybrid</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{LW}}=\frac{1}{\ell}\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}\bigl\|F_{i}^{\mathrm{Transformer}}(\mathbf{X}_{i-1}^{\mathrm{Transformer}})-F_{i}^{\mathrm{Hybrid}}(\mathbf{X}_{i-1}^{\mathrm{Hybrid}})\bigr\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This provides denser supervision by constraining each SSM layer to align with the Attention layer’s output at the same depth. Note that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> includes both the sequence mixer and the MLP so the alignment operates at the full decoder-layer level, giving the MLP room to compensate for approximation errors in the SSM.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We ablate both objectives in <a href="#S5.SS3.SSS2" title="5.3.2 Stage 1: End-to-end vs. Layerwise MSE ‣ 5.3 Priming Pipeline Ablation ‣ 5 Priming: Main Results ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3.2</span></a> and find that end-to-end MSE outperforms the layerwise variant. Consequently, Primed Hybrid models use end-to-end MSE for Stage 1 alignment unless otherwise specified.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.5 </span>Stage 2: Task Adaptation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After Stage 1, the Hybrid model retains the source Transformer’s high <a href="#S3.Ex1" title="Equation Hankel rank ‣ 3.1.2 The Realization Theory Problem ‣ 3.1 Realization-theoretic Foundations for Attention-to-SSM Priming ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Hankel rank</span></a> Attention layers unchanged and replaces the remaining layers with SSMs trained to <span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">realize</span> their low-rank Attention counterparts. Stage 2 takes this standalone model and fine-tunes it end-to-end on the downstream task of interest using standard cross-entropy next-token prediction. In this work we consider two recipes, both starting from the Stage 1 Hybrid model.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS5.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Long-context Instruct model.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first extend the model’s effective context length to 128K via continued pretraining on long-context data. We then perform supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on instruction-following data to produce a general-purpose long-context instruct model. The full training pipeline for our instruct-tuned models is detailed in <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Instruction Tuning ‣ 10 Training Recipes ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS5.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Long-context Reasoning model.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first perform reasoning SFT at 32K context on chain-of-thought traces. We then extend the context to 128K using a mixture of reasoning traces and long documents, and finally align the model to the instruction format via a long-context instruction-alignment phase. The full training pipeline for our reasoning models is detailed in <a href="#S10.SS3" title="10.3 Long-Context Reasoning ‣ 10 Training Recipes ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.3</span></a>.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>SSM Layers for Fading Memory in Hybrids</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now describe the specific sequence-mixing layers we use to realize Attention in our Primed Hybrid models. We consider four candidates, the pure-SSM families Mamba-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao and Gu, 2024</a>)</cite>, GDN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, and GKA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, along with B’MOJO-F <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zancato et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which is a Hybrid layer that couples an SSM with Sliding Window Attention inside a single sublayer. We describe the three pure-SSM families next through a common lens that highlights their differences in expressivity and state-update dynamics in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 SSM Layer Types ‣ 4 SSM Layers for Fading Memory in Hybrids ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. GKA receives additional treatment in <a href="#S8" title="8 Gated KalmaNet: A Fading Memory SSM Layer with Variable Test-time Compute ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, owing to its distinctive property of exposing a runtime compute-quality knob that can be adjusted at inference time without retraining. B’MOJO-F, being a Hybrid layer, is discussed separately in <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Hybrid Layers: B’MOJO-F ‣ 4 SSM Layers for Fading Memory in Hybrids ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>;</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>SSM Layer Types</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The three SSM layer types we consider share the generic recurrence <a href="#S2.E2" title="In State Space Models (SSMs). ‣ 2.1 Preliminaries: Attention and State Space Models ‣ 2 Hybrid Architectures ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, differing only in the structure of the transition operator <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the gating operator <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. These structural differences determine how each layer manages its finite memory. We describe the three choices below and summarize their transition structures in <a href="#S4.T1" title="In Expressiveness hierarchy. ‣ 4.1 SSM Layer Types ‣ 4 SSM Layers for Fading Memory in Hybrids ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mamba-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao and Gu, 2024</a>)</cite>.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mamba-2 can be represented as the simplest state update (up to specific parametrization): an input-dependent scalar decay applied
uniformly to the entire state, combined with a direct key write,</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}=\gamma_{t}\,\mathbf{I},\qquad\bm{B}_{t}=\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad\mathbf{S}_{t}=\gamma_{t}\,\mathbf{S}_{t-1}+\bm{v}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>𝑩</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒌</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mi>𝐒</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>𝐒</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒗</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>𝒌</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}=\gamma_{t}\,\mathbf{I},\qquad\bm{B}_{t}=\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad\mathbf{S}_{t}=\gamma_{t}\,\mathbf{S}_{t-1}+\bm{v}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a scalar decay computed from the current input token <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> through a learned projection and nonlinearity. Because <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a scalar multiple of the identity, every past key-value association is progressively forgotten as more associations are observed regardless of their content.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Gated DeltaNet (GDN) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GDN augments Mamba-2’s uniform decay with a <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single-key erase</em> based on the <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">delta update rule</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Widrow and Hoff, 1960</a>)</cite>: before writing the new value, it subtracts from the state a rank-1 term aligned with the current key, selectively removing the value previously associated with that key,</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}=\gamma_{t}\bigl(\mathbf{I}-\beta_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\bigr),\qquad\bm{B}_{t}=\beta_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\,\gamma_{t}\bigl(\mathbf{I}-\beta_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\bigr)+\beta_{t}\,\bm{v}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}=\gamma_{t}\bigl(\mathbf{I}-\beta_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\bigr),\qquad\bm{B}_{t}=\beta_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\,\gamma_{t}\bigl(\mathbf{I}-\beta_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\bigr)+\beta_{t}\,\bm{v}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t},\beta_{t}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t},\beta_{t}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> are scalar factors computed from the current input token <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> through learned projections and nonlinearities, with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the scalar decay inherited from Mamba-2 and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the strength of the key-specific erase. Whereas Mamba-2’s <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a pure scalar multiple of the identity, GDN’s <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> has the structure <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">scalar-times-identity plus a rank-1 correction</em>, so GDN can erase along a specific key direction without applying equally strong decay elsewhere. In the limit <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>, global decay vanishes and the update becomes a pure key-specific revision; in general, GDN interpolates between uniform decay and key-specific revision at each step. This targeted update makes GDN strictly more expressive than Mamba-2.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Gated KalmaNet (GKA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GKA (pronounced "gee-ka") retains GDN’s scalar-times-identity-plus-rank-1 structure, but replaces GDN’s <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single-key erase</em> with a <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">history-aware erase</em> that accounts for every key seen so far. Concretely, the rank-1 erase direction is no longer aligned with the current key alone; instead, it is
an input-varying vector <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{t}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{t}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> that
depends on all past keys <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{k}_{1},\ldots,\bm{k}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{k}_{1},\ldots,\bm{k}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}=\mathbf{I}-\bm{k}_{t}\,\mathbf{g}_{t}^{\top},\qquad\bm{B}_{t}=\mathbf{g}_{t}^{\top}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\bigl(\mathbf{I}-\bm{k}_{t}\,\mathbf{g}_{t}^{\top}\bigr)+\bm{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{g}_{t}^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}=\mathbf{I}-\bm{k}_{t}\,\mathbf{g}_{t}^{\top},\qquad\bm{B}_{t}=\mathbf{g}_{t}^{\top}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\bigl(\mathbf{I}-\bm{k}_{t}\,\mathbf{g}_{t}^{\top}\bigr)+\bm{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{g}_{t}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The vector <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained as a function of all the past keys as follows<span id="footnote8" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">8</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              Note, the state update form in Equation (KF) from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al. (2025)</a></cite> is in terms of <math id="footnote8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Phi}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Phi}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> which can be obtained using the Sherman-Morrison identity that <math id="footnote8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Phi}_{t}=\bm{\Phi}_{t-1}-\dfrac{\beta_{t}\,\bm{\Phi}_{t-1}\,\bm{k}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\,\bm{\Phi}_{t-1}}{1+\beta_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\,\bm{\Phi}_{t-1}\,\bm{k}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Phi}_{t}=\bm{\Phi}_{t-1}-\dfrac{\beta_{t}\,\bm{\Phi}_{t-1}\,\bm{k}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\,\bm{\Phi}_{t-1}}{1+\beta_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}^{\top}\,\bm{\Phi}_{t-1}\,\bm{k}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{t}=\beta_{t}\,\bm{\Phi}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t},\qquad\bm{\Phi}_{t}\;:=\;\Big(\sum_{i\leq t}\beta_{i}\big(\prod_{j&gt;i}\gamma_{j}\big)\,\bm{k}_{i}\bm{k}_{i}^{\top}+\lambda_{t}\mathbf{I}\Big)^{-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{t}=\beta_{t}\,\bm{\Phi}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t},\qquad\bm{\Phi}_{t}\;:=\;\Big(\sum_{i\leq t}\beta_{i}\big(\prod_{j&gt;i}\gamma_{j}\big)\,\bm{k}_{i}\bm{k}_{i}^{\top}+\lambda_{t}\mathbf{I}\Big)^{-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a time-dependent regularizer for proper conditioning of the inverse matrix <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Phi}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Phi}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>, Equation 4)</cite>). We note that the input-selectivity parameter <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S4.E9" title="In Gated KalmaNet (GKA) ( , ). ‣ 4.1 SSM Layer Types ‣ 4 SSM Layers for Fading Memory in Hybrids ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> is not part of the original GKA formulation in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al. (2025)</a></cite>; we introduce it here, and develop the motivation and ablations in <a href="#S8.SS1" title="8.1 Input Selectivity for GKA via 𝛽_𝑡 ‣ 8 Gated KalmaNet: A Fading Memory SSM Layer with Variable Test-time Compute ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a>. The recurrence in <a href="#S4.E8" title="In Gated KalmaNet (GKA) ( , ). ‣ 4.1 SSM Layer Types ‣ 4 SSM Layers for Fading Memory in Hybrids ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>
originates from the Kalman Filter formulation of an online
ridge-regression problem, hence the name <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gated KalmaNet</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From <a href="#S4.E9" title="In Gated KalmaNet (GKA) ( , ). ‣ 4.1 SSM Layer Types ‣ 4 SSM Layers for Fading Memory in Hybrids ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, the erase direction <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{g}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{g}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the current key <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{k}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{k}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> pre-multiplied by the inverse of a regularized,
input-weighted cumulative sum of past outer products
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\leq t}\beta_{i}\big(\prod_{j&gt;i}\gamma_{j}\big)\,\bm{k}_{i}\bm{k}_{i}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\leq t}\beta_{i}\big(\prod_{j&gt;i}\gamma_{j}\big)\,\bm{k}_{i}\bm{k}_{i}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.This is precisely the sense in which <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{g}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{g}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on all past keys: past tokens that wrote weakly (small <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) or long ago (small <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{j&gt;i}\gamma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{j&gt;i}\gamma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>) contribute little to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Phi}_{t}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Phi}_{t}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and, through the inverse<span id="footnote9" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">9</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              To the extent that their key directions are not already well-represented by other past keys.</span></span></span>, leave <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Phi}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Phi}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> large along their key directions; conversely, tokens that wrote strongly or are recent, shrink <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Phi}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Phi}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> along
their directions. Since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{g}_{t}=\beta_{t}\,\bm{\Phi}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{g}_{t}=\beta_{t}\,\bm{\Phi}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the erase direction
inherits this structure from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Phi}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Phi}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the contribution of each past
direction further modulated by its alignment with the current key <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{k}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{k}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, Mamba-2 decays all state directions uniformly by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and GDN combines the same uniform decay with a rank-1 erase along the current key <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{k}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{k}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>; GKA’s erase direction is instead shaped by the entire key history.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Expressiveness hierarchy.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The three layers form a progression in how the rank-1 correction in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> uses the state: Mamba-2 has no correction and simply applies a uniform scalar decay (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}=\gamma_{t}\,\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}=\gamma_{t}\,\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math>); GDN adds a rank-1 correction aligned with the current key alone
(<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}=\gamma_{t}(\mathbf{I}-\beta_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}\bm{k}_{t}^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}=\gamma_{t}(\mathbf{I}-\beta_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}\bm{k}_{t}^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math>); GKA replaces that single-key correction with one whose direction <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{g}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{g}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on all past keys
(<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}=\mathbf{I}-\bm{k}_{t}\,\bm{g}_{t}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}=\mathbf{I}-\bm{k}_{t}\,\bm{g}_{t}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>). This yields the expressiveness hierarchy
GKA <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> GDN <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> Mamba-2, with all three sharing the fading-memory property of a bounded state; they differ only in how selectively that state is updated at each step.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Structural differences between the SSM layers considered in this work, expressed through the transition operator <math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the write operator <math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of the generic recurrence <math id="S4.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\mathbf{A}_{t}+\bm{v}_{t}\,\bm{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\mathbf{A}_{t}+\bm{v}_{t}\,\bm{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For GKA, <math id="S4.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{g}_{t}=\beta_{t}\,\bm{\Phi}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{g}_{t}=\beta_{t}\,\bm{\Phi}_{t}\,\bm{k}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Phi}_{t}=\bigl(\sum_{i\leq t}\beta_{i}\prod_{j&gt;i}\gamma_{j}\,\bm{k}_{i}\bm{k}_{i}^{\top}+\lambda_{t}\mathbf{I}\bigr)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.330em" minsize="1.330em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.330em" minsize="1.330em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Phi}_{t}=\bigl(\sum_{i\leq t}\beta_{i}\prod_{j&gt;i}\gamma_{j}\,\bm{k}_{i}\bm{k}_{i}^{\top}+\lambda_{t}\mathbf{I}\bigr)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> the regularized, input-weighted inverse of past key outer products (see Eq. 4 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al. (2025)</a></cite>).</span></figcaption>
