</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S10.T12.7" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><sup id="S10.T12.7.1" class="ltx_sup"><math id="S10.T12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dagger" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.800em">†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dagger</annotation></semantics></math></sup><span id="S10.T12.7.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10B tokens for 32B model scale; 25B tokens for 8B model scale.
<span id="S10.T12.7.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p></div></div>
</figure>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">10.3.1 </span>Multi-stage Training For Long-context Reasoning</h4>

<figure id="S10.F18" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.08301v1/figures/reasoning_recipe.jpeg" id="S10.F18.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/55;" width="381" height="55" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S10.F18.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 18</span>: </span><span id="S10.F18.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Our multi-stage long-context reasoning pipeline.<span id="S10.F18.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Stages 0+1 of the Priming procedure allow our Hybrid architectures to sidestep pre-training costs by leveraging a pre-trained Transformer model. Stage 2 for reasoning consists of three phases. (i) <span id="S10.F18.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Short-context SFT</span> consists of SFT on packed reasoning examples at 32K context. Next, (ii) a <span id="S10.F18.8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">context-extension</span> phase where we train on a mix of reasoning samples and long-context documents to extend the context from 32K to 128K. Finally, (iii) we perform a <span id="S10.F18.8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">long instruction-alignment</span> phase where we train on packed reasoning samples at 128K context to align the model to the instruct-format seen during deployment.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S10.F18" title="Figure 18 ‣ 10.3.1 Multi-stage Training For Long-context Reasoning ‣ 10.3 Long-Context Reasoning ‣ 10 Training Recipes ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> depicts our multi-stage reasoning pipeline. Next we describe in detail the three phases for our Stage 2 recipe for reasoning models.</p>
</div>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Short-context SFT.</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train on packed reasoning traces at 32K context, filtering conversations to <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>32K tokens to avoid truncation. Samples are concatenated using simple example packing with no Attention masking or state reset (for SSM layers) between sequences.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Context-extension.</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This phase extends the context length of our Primed Hybrid models from 32K to 128K. We train on a mixture of reasoning traces in chat SFT format and long documents in pre-training format<span id="footnote30" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">30</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">30</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">30</span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                Pre-training format is tokenization of samples without any chat template, that is, no prompt-response chat structure or any special role tokens.</span></span></span> at 128K context length. The model is trained using a heterogeneous loss where tokens corresponding to reasoning traces receive the SFT loss by masking out prompt tokens, whereas the tokens belonging to the long documents obtain the next-token-prediction loss (without any masking, as is standard in pre-training). Mixing pre-training and chat SFT data requires separate end-of-sequence delimiters for the two data-types. Without this
separation, the model may output text that mimics pre-training documents or produce excessively long reasoning chains for simple problems. In our experiments, we use the <span id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;|endoftext|&gt;</span> token from the Qwen3 tokenizer as the delimiter for pre-training documents and <span id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;|im_start|&gt;</span>
&amp; <span id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;|im_end|&gt;</span> delimiters (between turns) for SFT reasoning traces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure sufficient coverage of long samples in the training mix, longer reasoning traces are up-sampled via a length-weighted formula. Specifically, given <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> different document sources each with a sampling probability <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the average conversation length for document source <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{L}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the probability-weighted average conversation length across all sources. We modify the original sampling probability <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of source <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to get modified sampling probability <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S10.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{i}=\frac{p_{i}\bar{L}_{i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}p_{j}\bar{L}_{j}}=p_{i}\Big(\frac{\bar{L}_{i}}{\bar{L}}\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{i}=\frac{p_{i}\bar{L}_{i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}p_{j}\bar{L}_{j}}=p_{i}\Big(\frac{\bar{L}_{i}}{\bar{L}}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This has the effect of up-weighting document sources that have higher average length <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> than the global average <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Long instruction alignment.</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the context-extension phase consists of a heterogeneous mix of chat SFT data and pre-training data, we perform a final training stage to align the model to the chat format it will be required to have during deployment. In this final long-instruction-alignment phase, we perform SFT on packed reasoning traces at 128K context.

<br class="ltx_break"> 
<br class="ltx_break">The total token count across all stages of our reasoning pipeline is approximately 150B tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">10.3.2 </span>Reasoning Data Mix Construction</h4>

<figure id="S10.F19" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.08301v1/reasoning_data_dist.svg" id="S10.F19.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/130;" width="381" height="130"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S10.F19.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 19</span>: </span><span id="S10.F19.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">We depict the document and token ratios of our reasoning SFT data mixture by domain.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our reasoning traces consist of curated chain-of-thought traces across various domains, including math, coding, agentic/tool-calling, science, and chat/instruct. The document and token ratios by domain are depicted in <a href="#S10.F19" title="In 10.3.2 Reasoning Data Mix Construction ‣ 10.3 Long-Context Reasoning ‣ 10 Training Recipes ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>. Constructing a data mixture of high-quality reasoning traces requires several filtering and balancing steps, each addressing a specific failure mode. We describe these below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Filtering out degenerate reasoning traces</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Degenerate reasoning traces like loops, stutters and repetitive chains of thought that would otherwise decrease the signal-to-noise ratio in training are filtered our using repeated n-gram filtering within a sliding window.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Controlling the tool use frequency</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the final data mix, <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>70</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80\%</annotation></semantics></math> of samples with tool definitions actually
invoke tools. Our raw reasoning math data contained Python tool usage without
corresponding definitions in the system prompt. These were corrected by inserting appropriate tool definitions, not doing so causes either tool hallucination or tool use in situations where it is not appropriate.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Invalid Python use filtering.</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A small fraction of math-domain samples (<math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.89%) contained code blocks despite no code-execution tool being defined in the conversation. This caused the model to answer math problems with <span id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">print()</span> statements rather than mathematical reasoning. These erroneous samples were filtered out.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Random packing over greedy packing.</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Common implementations of greedy (best-fit) packing bias longer reasoning traces toward the beginning
of the context window (on average, the first reasoning trace in a sample was <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>1K tokens longer). Random packing
removes this positional bias, which is important for heterogeneous data mixes
where trace lengths vary widely.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Packed reasoning is insufficient for long-context.</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training on packed reasoning samples at 128K context, where individual
reasoning traces max out at <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}32</annotation></semantics></math>K tokens, causes long-context performance to severely degrade beyond 32K. Despite being exposed to 128K tokens, the model has no incentive to retain memory in the state across document boundaries when trained in this fashion. Consequentially, long documents and reasoning traces must be included in the training mix to induce long-context recall capabilities. This finding motivates the dedicated context extension phase described in <a href="#S10.SS3.SSS1" title="10.3.1 Multi-stage Training For Long-context Reasoning ‣ 10.3 Long-Context Reasoning ‣ 10 Training Recipes ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S11" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">11 </span>Inference and Deployment</h2>

<div id="S11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hybrid models have a fundamentally different memory structure from Transformers. A Transformer’s sequence-mixing layers are exclusively eidetic: every layer maintains a KV cache that stores each token’s representation exactly, at a cost that grows linearly with context length. A Hybrid model mixes eidetic layers (Attention) with fading memory layers (SSMs), whose recurrent state is fixed-size regardless of context length (<a href="#S2" title="2 Hybrid Architectures ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). This structural difference induces different per-layer costs with respect to context length, and its impact on inference efficiency becomes concrete when serving a Primed Hybrid model alongside its source Transformer. We use vLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2023</a>)</cite> as our production inference engine: its plugin system allows us to register custom model architectures without forking the codebase, which is essential for supporting the novel layer types introduced by Priming while continuing to benefit from upstream improvements.<span id="footnote31" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">31</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">31</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">31</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Our vLLM plugin registers Primed Hybrid architectures via vLLM’s entry-point system, preserving continuous batching, chunked prefill, prefix caching, and tensor parallelism over both Attention and SSM layers.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The dynamics differ between the two phases of autoregressive serving. Prefill, processing the input prompt, is primarily compute-bound; our Primed Hybrid models see moderate gains here by replacing quadratic Attention computations with linear-cost SSM recurrences. Decode, generating tokens one at a time, is primarily memory-bandwidth-bound, dominated by reading KV cache entries from GPU memory; this is where the Primed Hybrid’s reduced memory footprint
translates most directly into throughput. The decode phase dominates wall-clock time in the workloads we
target. In mathematical reasoning, long chains of thought produce far more output tokens than the input prompt. In agentic coding, conversations are multi-turn with large tool outputs, but prefix caching ensures that only newly appended tokens require prefilling each turn; cumulative decode, the total time spent generating tokens across a session, far exceeds cumulative prefill. As a reference point, a single full 128K-context prefill on Qwen3-32B costs roughly the same wall-clock time as generating just <math id="S11.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}1{,}500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>1,500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}1{,}500</annotation></semantics></math> tokens at that context length, a small fraction of the cumulative decode in a typical reasoning trace or multi-turn agentic session.</p>
</div>
<section id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">A model of decode throughput.</h5>

<div id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One of the principal benefits of Hybrid models is their inference efficiency, inherited directly from the SSM memory layers described in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 SSM Layer Types ‣ 4 SSM Layers for Fading Memory in Hybrids ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. Before deciding which SSM type to instantiate and which Hybrid ratio to target via Priming, it is useful to have a simple predictive model of the expected throughput improvement. We develop such a model under KV-cache saturation, the production serving regime in which the GPU’s available memory is fully occupied by KV cache entries, so that serving additional requests requires evicting existing state or queuing, and show that it explains the pattern of speedups reported in <a href="#S11.T13" title="In 32B models. ‣ 11.1 Decode Throughput ‣ 11 Inference and Deployment ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>–<a href="#S11.T14" title="In 8B models. ‣ 11.1 Decode Throughput ‣ 11 Inference and Deployment ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a Transformer serving requests at KV-cache saturation on a fixed GPU: <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> concurrent sequences, each with context length <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> Attention layers. Each decode step produces one token per sequence, so the model produces <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> tokens per forward pass. At saturation the KV cache is proportional to <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\cdot n\cdot l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\cdot n\cdot l</annotation></semantics></math>, and the product <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="bl" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">bl</annotation></semantics></math> is near constant, doubling the context length requires approximately halving the batch size.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Hybrid ratio, i.e. the fraction of the <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> sequence-mixing layers being SSM layers. Our Primed Hybrid models have <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rn</annotation></semantics></math> SSM layers whose recurrent state is fixed-size (i.e. independent of <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1{-}r)n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1{-}r)n</annotation></semantics></math> Attention layers. At the Transformer’s saturated batch size <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hybrid’s KV cache occupancy is only <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\cdot(1{-}r)n\cdot l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\cdot(1{-}r)n\cdot l</annotation></semantics></math>, a fraction <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1{-}r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1{-}r)</annotation></semantics></math> of the total budget, leaving a fraction <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> unused. Filling this headroom allows the Hybrid to serve a larger batch <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\approx}\,b/(1{-}r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.448em">≈</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\approx}\,b/(1{-}r)</annotation></semantics></math> on identical hardware. The <em id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">aggregate</em> KV cache traffic per decode step remains approximately unchanged:</p>
<table id="S11.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underbrace{\frac{b}{1-r}\cdot(1{-}r)n\cdot l}_{\text{Hybrid}}\;\approx\;\underbrace{b\cdot n\cdot l}_{\text{Transformer}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Hybrid</mtext></munder><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Transformer</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underbrace{\frac{b}{1-r}\cdot(1{-}r)n\cdot l}_{\text{Hybrid}}\;\approx\;\underbrace{b\cdot n\cdot l}_{\text{Transformer}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The approximation neglects the cost of reading each SSM layer’s fixed-size recurrent state, equivalent to at most <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>K tokens of KV cache (for our Primed Hybrid models), which becomes negligible at modest context lengths. While the aggregate memory utilization is approximately equal, the two architectures distribute this traffic differently across layers: the Transformer makes <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> Attention calls each serving batch <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, while the Primed Hybrid makes <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1{-}r)n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1{-}r)n</annotation></semantics></math> Attention calls each serving the larger batch.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A decode forward pass comprises three main cost components. Writing <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}=b/(1{-}r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}=b/(1{-}r)</annotation></semantics></math> for the Primed Hybrid batch size:</p>
<ol id="S11.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S11.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S11.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S11.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Attention layers</em>, dominated by KV cache reads;
per-layer cost depends on both batch size and context length. The
Transformer has <math id="S11.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> such layers, our Primed Hybrid has <math id="S11.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1{-}r)n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1{-}r)n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S11.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S11.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MLP layers</em>, dominated by weight reads; cost scales
with batch size, independent of context length. All <math id="S11.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> MLP layers
are present in both architectures.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S11.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S11.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SSM layers</em>, state update cost scales with batch size,
independent of context length. Present only in our Primed Hybrid
(<math id="S11.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rn</annotation></semantics></math> layers).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b,l)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\mathrm{mlp}}(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\mathrm{mlp}}(b)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\mathrm{ssm}}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ssm</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\mathrm{ssm}}(B)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the per-layer wall-clock time of a single Attention, MLP, and SSM layer respectively. The aggregate forward-pass times for the source Transformer (<math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathrm{ST}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>ST</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathrm{ST}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and our Primed Hybrid (<math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathrm{PH}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>PH</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathrm{PH}}</annotation></semantics></math>) are then:</p>
<table id="S11.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S11.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S11.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathrm{ST}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>ST</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathrm{ST}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S11.E22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=n\,t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b,l)+n\,t_{\mathrm{mlp}}(b)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=n\,t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b,l)+n\,t_{\mathrm{mlp}}(b),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S11.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S11.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathrm{PH}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>PH</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathrm{PH}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S11.E23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(1{-}r)n\,t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b^{\prime},l)+n\,t_{\mathrm{mlp}}(b^{\prime})+rn\,t_{\mathrm{ssm}}(b^{\prime})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ssm</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(1{-}r)n\,t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b^{\prime},l)+n\,t_{\mathrm{mlp}}(b^{\prime})+rn\,t_{\mathrm{ssm}}(b^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The Transformer produces <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> tokens per step and our Primed Hybrid produces <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The decode throughput ratio is therefore</p>
<table id="S11.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\;=\;\frac{b^{\prime}}{b}\,\frac{T_{\mathrm{ST}}}{T_{\mathrm{PH}}}\;\approx\;\frac{1}{1{-}r}\,\frac{t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b,l)+t_{\mathrm{mlp}}(b)}{(1{-}r)t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b^{\prime},l)+t_{\mathrm{mlp}}(b^{\prime})+rt_{\mathrm{ssm}}(b^{\prime})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>ST</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>PH</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ssm</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\;=\;\frac{b^{\prime}}{b}\,\frac{T_{\mathrm{ST}}}{T_{\mathrm{PH}}}\;\approx\;\frac{1}{1{-}r}\,\frac{t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b,l)+t_{\mathrm{mlp}}(b)}{(1{-}r)t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b^{\prime},l)+t_{\mathrm{mlp}}(b^{\prime})+rt_{\mathrm{ssm}}(b^{\prime})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Context-length dependence.</h5>

<div id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At large <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, each Attention layer reads a KV cache proportional to <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, while MLP weight reads and SSM state updates remain fixed regardless of <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>. The per layer Attention cost <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\mathrm{attn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\mathrm{attn}}</annotation></semantics></math> therefore dominates the forward pass, and the MLP and SSM contributions become negligible. In this regime the ratio simplifies to</p>
<table id="S11.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\;\xrightarrow{}\;\frac{1}{1{-}r}\,\frac{t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b,l)}{(1{-}r)t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b^{\prime},l)}\;=\;\frac{1}{(1{-}r)^{2}}\,\frac{t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b,l)}{t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b^{\prime},l)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0.558em">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\;\xrightarrow{}\;\frac{1}{1{-}r}\,\frac{t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b,l)}{(1{-}r)t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b^{\prime},l)}\;=\;\frac{1}{(1{-}r)^{2}}\,\frac{t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b,l)}{t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b^{\prime},l)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The limiting throughput ratio is determined entirely by how the single-layer Attention forward pass scales with batch size. If <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\mathrm{attn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\mathrm{attn}}</annotation></semantics></math> scales linearly in <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> (scaling the batch by <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(1{-}r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(1{-}r)</annotation></semantics></math> scales the time equally), then <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\to 1/(1{-}r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to 1/(1{-}r)</annotation></semantics></math>. If the scaling is sub-linear, the Attention kernel becomes more efficient at larger batch sizes, for example through better HBM bandwidth utilization, then <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b^{\prime},l)&lt;t_{\mathrm{attn}}(B,L)/(1{-}r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\mathrm{attn}}(b^{\prime},l)&lt;t_{\mathrm{attn}}(B,L)/(1{-}r)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R&gt;1/(1{-}r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R&gt;1/(1{-}r)</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, super-linear scaling would yield <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R&lt;1/(1{-}r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R&lt;1/(1{-}r)</annotation></semantics></math>. All Primed Hybrid models in this paper use <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1/2</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}\approx 2b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}\approx 2b</annotation></semantics></math> and the linear-scaling baseline is <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\to 1/(1{-}r)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to 1/(1{-}r)=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Our implementation uses FlashAttention-3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for all Attention layers; the empirical observation of <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R=2.26\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.26</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=2.26\times</annotation></semantics></math> at 128K indicates sub-linear scaling of this kernel at the operating points tested.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At short <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, the batch <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is large and the MLP and SSM terms are no longer negligible, they add cost to <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathrm{PH}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>PH</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathrm{PH}}</annotation></semantics></math> without a corresponding increase in <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathrm{ST}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>ST</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathrm{ST}}</annotation></semantics></math>, pulling <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> below the Attention-determined limit. This explains the observed pattern: <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R=1.86\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.86</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=1.86\times</annotation></semantics></math> at 16K growing to <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R=2.26\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.26</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=2.26\times</annotation></semantics></math> at 128K for our 8B GKA-Primed-HQwen3 model (<a href="#S11.T14" title="In 8B models. ‣ 11.1 Decode Throughput ‣ 11 Inference and Deployment ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). At very short contexts (<math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\lesssim 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≲</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\lesssim 4</annotation></semantics></math>K), <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> can drop below <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> due to two compounding effects: the SSM layers carry fixed-size recurrent state equivalent to at most <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>K tokens of KV cache, which at short <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> erodes the batch-size advantage; and FlashAttention-3’s highly optimized short-context kernels give the source Transformer a lower per-token decode cost at these short contexts. In practice this regime is negligible: the workloads we target quickly accumulate context well beyond <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>K, after which the Primed Hybrid’s throughput advantage takes hold and grows with context length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We note that these gains are a function of the Attention kernel implementations and hardware. Future kernel improvements on efficiency at large batch sizes, strengthening the sub-linear trend, would directly increase the Primed Hybrid throughput advantage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S11.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">11.1 </span>Decode Throughput</h3>

<section id="S11.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">32B models.</h5>

<div id="S11.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S11.T13" title="In 32B models. ‣ 11.1 Decode Throughput ‣ 11 Inference and Deployment ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> reports sustained decode throughput on
<math id="S11.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="8\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\times</annotation></semantics></math> H200 GPUs (TP=8) under KV-cache saturation. GDN-Primed-HQwen3 reaches
<math id="S11.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.11\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.11</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.11\times</annotation></semantics></math> at 128K, and GKA-Primed-HQwen3, whose symmetric-tiled decode kernel is detailed in <a href="#S8.SS3" title="8.3 A Symmetric Tiled Triton Kernel for GKA Decoding ‣ 8 Gated KalmaNet: A Fading Memory SSM Layer with Variable Test-time Compute ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.3</span></a>, reaches <math id="S11.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.02\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.02</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.02\times</annotation></semantics></math> at 128K at the default
<span id="S11.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">num_iter</span>=30, and <math id="S11.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.06\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.06</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.06\times</annotation></semantics></math> at <span id="S11.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">num_iter</span>=10. GKA’s throughput can be further modulated via the variable test-time compute tradeoff described in <a href="#S8.SS2" title="8.2 Variable Test-time Compute with GKA ‣ 8 Gated KalmaNet: A Fading Memory SSM Layer with Variable Test-time Compute ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S11.T13" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S11.T13.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 13</span>: </span><span id="S11.T13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Measured decode throughput (tokens/s) for our 32B Primed Hybrid models on <math id="S11.T13.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="8\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\times</annotation></semantics></math> H200
(TP=8). Speedup over Qwen3-32B in parentheses.
</span></figcaption>
