<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Priming Implementation Details</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Gate Initialization</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SSM layers include an output gate <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{t}=\mathbf{W}_{G}\mathbf{X}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{t}=\mathbf{W}_{G}\mathbf{X}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that modulates the layer’s output before the final projection. Prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>]</cite> initializes <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> randomly. We instead initialize it as a blend of the transposed output projection and the GQA-expanded value projection:</p>
<table id="A1.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{G}=0.5\cdot\bigl(\mathbf{W}_{O}^{\top}+\texttt{repeat\_interleave}(\mathbf{W}_{V},\,m)\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">repeat_interleave</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{G}=0.5\cdot\bigl(\mathbf{W}_{O}^{\top}+\texttt{repeat\_interleave}(\mathbf{W}_{V},\,m)\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=H_{Q}/H_{\mathrm{KV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>KV</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=H_{Q}/H_{\mathrm{KV}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the GQA group size and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{repeat\_interleave}(\mathbf{W}_{V},m)\in\mathbb{R}^{H_{Q}\cdot d_{\mathrm{head}}\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">repeat_interleave</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>head</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{repeat\_interleave}(\mathbf{W}_{V},m)\in\mathbb{R}^{H_{Q}\cdot d_{\mathrm{head}}\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math> repeats each of the <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathrm{KV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>KV</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathrm{KV}}</annotation></semantics></math> value heads <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> times to match the shape of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{O}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{O}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. The gate controls how much of the recurrent output reaches the output projection, making it dependent on both the output space structure and the input content. <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{O}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{O}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> provides alignment with the output space; the expanded <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the input content structure. Initializing the gate in this manner allows our Primed Hybrid models to effectively use the knowledge stored in the pre-trained weights of the source transformer rather than learning from scratch. We apply this initialization uniformly across GKA, GDN, and Mamba2.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F20" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.08301v1/gate_init_ablation.svg" id="A1.F20.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/159;" width="238" height="159"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F20.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 20</span>: </span><span id="A1.F20.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of the primed gate initialization on long-context performance.<span id="A1.F20.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Per-context-length scores of Mamba2-Primed-HQwen3-IT at 8B scale, with (green) and without (red) the gate initialization from <a href="#A1.E26" title="In A.1 Gate Initialization ‣ Appendix A Priming Implementation Details ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>. The “w/o primed gate” variant follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. [2024]</a></cite> and initializes <math id="A1.F20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> randomly. Both variants use the same Stage 0/1/2 recipe and differ only in <math id="A1.F20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> initialization. Scores are the average over long-context tasks discussed in <a href="#S5.SS1.SSS1" title="5.1.1 Long-context Evaluation ‣ 5.1 Primed Instruct Model Results ‣ 5 Priming: Main Results ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1.1</span></a> reported per context length.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Empirical validation.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A1.F20" title="In A.1 Gate Initialization ‣ Appendix A Priming Implementation Details ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> confirms that this effective use of pre-trained knowledge translates into measurable long-context gains on Mamba2-Primed-HQwen3-IT 8B. The two variants are competitive at 8K, and the gap grows with context length, reaching a 9.9% (relative) improvement of the primed gate over random initialization at 128K. Random initialization forces the gate to learn a good configuration from scratch during Stage 1 and Stage 2, and the Priming token budget (<math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.5% of the source Transformer’s pre-training budget) is not enough to fully close the gap. We validate this on Mamba2 and apply the same initialization to GKA and GDN, since the mechanism is architecture-agnostic.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>QK Norm Transfer</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Some Transformer architectures (e.g., Qwen3) apply normalization to the Q and K projections before computing attention. Standard SSM layers do not natively include these norms. Since Stage 0 seeds SSM parameters from the Attention projections, we add corresponding norm layers to the SSM architecture and initialize them with the source Transformer’s QK norm weights. This ensures the SSM layers operate in the same normalized space as the Attention layers from which they were initialized.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Adaptive GQA (AGQA)</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS3" title="3.2.3 State Expansion via Adaptive GQA ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.3</span></a>, standard GQA head repetition is lossy for SSM layers because identical keys and values produce identical state updates across grouped heads. AGQA replaces the fixed repetition with a learned low-rank projection plus a residual connection:</p>
<table id="A1.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathrm{AGQA}}(\mathbf{X})=\operatorname{\texttt{mat}}\bigl(\mathbf{W}_{2}\,\sigma(\mathbf{W}_{1}\operatorname{\texttt{vec}}(\mathbf{X}))\bigr)+\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{AGQA}}\,\mathbf{X}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>AGQA</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">mat</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">vec</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>AGQA</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathrm{AGQA}}(\mathbf{X})=\operatorname{\texttt{mat}}\bigl(\mathbf{W}_{2}\,\sigma(\mathbf{W}_{1}\operatorname{\texttt{vec}}(\mathbf{X}))\bigr)+\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{AGQA}}\,\mathbf{X},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the rank of the low-rank projection, <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times H_{\mathrm{KV}}\cdot d_{\mathrm{head}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>KV</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>head</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times H_{\mathrm{KV}}\cdot d_{\mathrm{head}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{H_{Q}\cdot d_{\mathrm{head}}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>head</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{H_{Q}\cdot d_{\mathrm{head}}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable parameters, <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is SiLU, and
<math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{AGQA}}\in\mathbb{R}^{H_{Q}\times H_{\mathrm{KV}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>AGQA</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>KV</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{AGQA}}\in\mathbb{R}^{H_{Q}\times H_{\mathrm{KV}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a residual matrix; <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\texttt{vec}}:\mathbb{R}^{H_{\mathrm{KV}}\times d_{\mathrm{head}}}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^{H_{\mathrm{KV}}\cdot d_{\mathrm{head}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">vec</mtext><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>KV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>head</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>KV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>head</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\texttt{vec}}:\mathbb{R}^{H_{\mathrm{KV}}\times d_{\mathrm{head}}}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^{H_{\mathrm{KV}}\cdot d_{\mathrm{head}}}</annotation></semantics></math> flattens to a vector, <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\texttt{mat}}:\mathbb{R}^{H_{Q}\cdot d_{\mathrm{head}}}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^{H_{Q}\times d_{\mathrm{head}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">mat</mtext><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>head</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>head</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\texttt{mat}}:\mathbb{R}^{H_{Q}\cdot d_{\mathrm{head}}}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^{H_{Q}\times d_{\mathrm{head}}}</annotation></semantics></math> reshapes to a matrix. We initialize <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to zeros and <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{AGQA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>AGQA</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{AGQA}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the standard GQA replication matrix
<math id="A1.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{GQA}}=\mathbf{I}_{H_{\mathrm{KV}}}\otimes\mathbf{1}_{m\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>GQA</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>KV</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{GQA}}=\mathbf{I}_{H_{\mathrm{KV}}}\otimes\mathbf{1}_{m\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathrm{AGQA}}=F_{\mathrm{GQA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>AGQA</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>GQA</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathrm{AGQA}}=F_{\mathrm{GQA}}</annotation></semantics></math> at the start of training. The model begins with standard GQA and learns to deviate from it only as needed. <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{AGQA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>AGQA</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{AGQA}}</annotation></semantics></math> can optionally be made learnable, allowing the model to adjust how KV heads are mixed across query groups beyond what the low-rank branch provides.
See <a href="#S5.SS3.SSS3" title="5.3.3 State Expansion: Adaptive GQA vs. GQA ‣ 5.3 Priming Pipeline Ablation ‣ 5 Priming: Main Results ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3.3</span></a> for experimental results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>B’MOJO-F Initialization</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">B’MOJO-F is a dual-stream layer that combines Sliding Window Attention (SWA) with a configurable SSM (GKA, GDN, or Mamba2) within a single decoder layer. During Stage 0, both components are initialized from the same source Attention layer: the SWA component receives a direct copy of <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the SSM component is initialized using the standard mapping described in <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS2" title="3.2.2 Stage 0: Transferring Knowledge from Source Transformer ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.2</span></a>. Both components are then trained independently during Stages 1 and 2.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Complete Initialization Mapping</h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A1.T17" title="In A.5 Complete Initialization Mapping ‣ Appendix A Priming Implementation Details ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> summarizes the full Stage 0 parameter transfer. For models with GQA, key and value heads are expanded via AGQA (<a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Adaptive GQA (AGQA) ‣ Appendix A Priming Implementation Details ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>) or standard <span id="A1.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">repeat_interleave</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T17" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A1.T17.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 17</span>: </span><span id="A1.T17.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Stage 0 initialization mapping from Attention projections to SSM parameters.<span id="A1.T17.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> For models with GQA, key and value heads are expanded via AGQA or standard head repetition. All remaining layer-specific parameters <math id="A1.T17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., convolutions, transition matrices) are initialized randomly.</span></span></figcaption>
<table id="A1.T17.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A1.T17.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T17.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T17.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SSM Parameter</span></th>
<td id="A1.T17.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T17.6.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialized From</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T17.6.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T17.6.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="A1.T17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A1.T17.6.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Attention <math id="A1.T17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T17.6.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T17.6.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="A1.T17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> (+ AGQA expansion)</th>
<td id="A1.T17.6.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Attention <math id="A1.T17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T17.6.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T17.6.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="A1.T17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> (+ AGQA expansion)</th>
<td id="A1.T17.6.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Attention <math id="A1.T17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T17.6.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T17.6.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="A1.T17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{O}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A1.T17.6.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Attention <math id="A1.T17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{O}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T17.6.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T17.6.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="A1.T17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A1.T17.6.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A1.T17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5\cdot(\mathbf{W}_{O}^{\top}+\texttt{repeat\_interleave}(\mathbf{W}_{V},m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">repeat_interleave</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5\cdot(\mathbf{W}_{O}^{\top}+\texttt{repeat\_interleave}(\mathbf{W}_{V},m))</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T17.6.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T17.6.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">QK Norms</th>
<td id="A1.T17.6.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Attention QK norms</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T17.6.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T17.6.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><math id="A1.T17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> (layer-specific)</th>
<td id="A1.T17.6.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Random</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Realization Theory</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Mixing Matrix and Finite-Horizon Input-Output Matrix</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All SSM layers considered in this work (Mamba-2, GDN, GKA) share the following general linear time-varying state-space form. Let <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the value input, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> the output at time <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> the matrix-valued latent state of state dimension <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. The dynamics equations are</p>
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})+\bm{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{B}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t}),\qquad\bm{y}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})+\bm{v}_{t}\,D_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})+\bm{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{B}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t}),\qquad\bm{y}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})+\bm{v}_{t}\,D_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{0}=\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{0}=\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the system matrices
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>
are all functions of the input sequence up to time <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> in their most general form.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Row-wise decomposition and Single-Input Single-Output (SISO) reduction.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The dynamics above simplify because the matrices <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are shared across all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> rows of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}^{(p)}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}^{(p)}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-th row of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{t})_{p},(y_{t})_{p}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{t})_{p},(y_{t})_{p}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinates of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the state update, the term <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the outer product <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math> times <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, so its <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-th row is simply <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{t})_{p}\,\mathbf{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{t})_{p}\,\mathbf{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the output, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}+\bm{v}_{t}\,D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}+\bm{v}_{t}\,D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{t})_{p}=\mathbf{S}_{t}^{(p)}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}+(v_{t})_{p}\,D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{t})_{p}=\mathbf{S}_{t}^{(p)}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}+(v_{t})_{p}\,D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Row by row, the dynamics therefore decouple into <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> independent scalar (SISO) systems, each driven by a different coordinate of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> but governed by the same matrices:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}^{(p)}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}^{(p)}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}+(v_{t})_{p}\,\mathbf{B}_{t},\qquad(y_{t})_{p}=\mathbf{S}_{t}^{(p)}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}+(v_{t})_{p}\,D_{t},\qquad p=1,\dots,d_{v}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}^{(p)}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}^{(p)}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}+(v_{t})_{p}\,\mathbf{B}_{t},\qquad(y_{t})_{p}=\mathbf{S}_{t}^{(p)}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}+(v_{t})_{p}\,D_{t},\qquad p=1,\dots,d_{v}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Because the finite horizon input-output matrix <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is identical for every coordinate <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, it suffices to analyse a single SISO channel. Writing <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t},y_{t}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t},y_{t}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for that coordinate and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> for the corresponding row of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the dynamics become</p>
<table id="A2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}=s_{t-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}+v_{t}\,\mathbf{B}_{t},\qquad y_{t}=s_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}+v_{t}\,D_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}=s_{t-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}+v_{t}\,\mathbf{B}_{t},\qquad y_{t}=s_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}+v_{t}\,D_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Unrolling the recurrence with <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}=\bm{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}=\bm{0}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</p>
<table id="A2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{i}=\sum_{j=1}^{i-1}v_{j}\;\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}\;+\;v_{i}\bigl(\mathbf{B}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}+D_{i}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=\sum_{j=1}^{i-1}v_{j}\;\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}\;+\;v_{i}\bigl(\mathbf{B}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}+D_{i}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The input-output relationship is <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{T}\,\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{T}\,\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=(y_{1},\dots,y_{T})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=(y_{1},\dots,y_{T})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}=(v_{1},\dots,v_{T})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}=(v_{1},\dots,v_{T})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finite horizon input-output matrix</em>:</p>
<table id="A2.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ij}=\begin{cases}\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}&amp;\text{if }i&gt;j,\\[6.0pt]
\mathbf{B}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}+D_{i}&amp;\text{if }i=j,\\[4.0pt]
0&amp;\text{if }i&lt;j.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ij}=\begin{cases}\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}&amp;\text{if }i&gt;j,\\[6.0pt]
\mathbf{B}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}+D_{i}&amp;\text{if }i=j,\\[4.0pt]
0&amp;\text{if }i&lt;j.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The matrix <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is lower-triangular by causality. Moreover, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semi-separable matrix of order <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></em>: every submatrix lying strictly below the diagonal has rank at most <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. To see why, note that the <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> entry for <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math> factors as <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{j}\cdot(\text{state-transition from }j\text{ to }i)\cdot\mathbf{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>state-transition from </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> to </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{j}\cdot(\text{state-transition from }j\text{ to }i)\cdot\mathbf{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the middle factor is <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math>; any subblock of such entries therefore has rank bounded by the state dimension <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. All SSMs considered in this work, Mamba-2, GDN, GKA, with state dimension <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> produce a finite horizon input-output matrix of this form, and the semi-separable order is the fundamental bottleneck that limits how faithfully an SSM can reproduce a given mixing matrix.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Finite-Horizon Per-Instance Realization of the Attention Map). ‣ 3.1.2 The Realization Theory Problem ‣ 3.1 Realization-theoretic Foundations for Attention-to-SSM Priming ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following result adapts the classical time-varying realization theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patrick Dewilde, 1998</a>, Theorem 3.7]</cite> to the causal Attention setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Finite-Horizon Per-Instance Realization of the Attention Map)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a sequence <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the sequence of values, and let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> be the causal Attention mixing matrix of a single head.
Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\leq t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\leq t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input sequence up to time <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a linear time-varying state-space realization</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})+\bm{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{B}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t}),\qquad\bm{y}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})+\bm{v}_{t}\,D_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})+\bm{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{B}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t}),\qquad\bm{y}_{t}=\mathbf{S}_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t})+\bm{v}_{t}\,D_{t}(\mathbf{X}_{\leq t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>, zero initial state, and state dimension <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\min}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\min}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, whose finite-horizon input-output matrix <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly the mixing matrix <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, no realization of this finite-horizon input-output map with smaller state dimension exists.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and write <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>. As noted above, the SISO reduction applies row-wise, so it suffices to work with the scalar system</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}=s_{t-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}+v_{t}\,\mathbf{B}_{t},\qquad y_{t}=s_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}+v_{t}\,D_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}=s_{t-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{t}+v_{t}\,\mathbf{B}_{t},\qquad y_{t}=s_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}+v_{t}\,D_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">with <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}=\bm{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}=\bm{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is causal, it is lower triangular.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finite horizon input-output matrix.</span> 
The induced input-output matrix <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is lower triangular with</p>
<table id="A2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T_{ii}=\mathbf{B}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}+D_{i},\qquad T_{ij}=\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i},\quad i&gt;j." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ii}=\mathbf{B}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}+D_{i},\qquad T_{ij}=\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i},\quad i&gt;j.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lower bound.</span> 
For every cut <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the past-to-future block satisfies</p>
<table id="A2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{T}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}=\bigl(\mathcal{R}_{k}\,\mathcal{O}_{k}\bigr)^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}=\bigl(\mathcal{R}_{k}\,\mathcal{O}_{k}\bigr)^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="A2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{k}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{B}_{1}\,\mathbf{A}_{2}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{k}\\[2.0pt]
\mathbf{B}_{2}\,\mathbf{A}_{3}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{k}\\
\vdots\\
\mathbf{B}_{k-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{k}\\[2.0pt]
\mathbf{B}_{k}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{k}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{B}_{1}\,\mathbf{A}_{2}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{k}\\[2.0pt]
\mathbf{B}_{2}\,\mathbf{A}_{3}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{k}\\
\vdots\\
\mathbf{B}_{k-1}\,\mathbf{A}_{k}\\[2.0pt]
\mathbf{B}_{k}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">is the reachability map (its <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th row is
<math id="A2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{j}\prod_{\ell=j+1}^{k}\mathbf{A}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{j}\prod_{\ell=j+1}^{k}\mathbf{A}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the empty product
equal to <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=k</annotation></semantics></math>), and</p>
<table id="A2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{k}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{A}_{k+1}\,\mathbf{C}_{k+1}&amp;\mathbf{A}_{k+1}\mathbf{A}_{k+2}\,\mathbf{C}_{k+2}&amp;\cdots&amp;\mathbf{A}_{k+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{T}\,\mathbf{C}_{T}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times(T-k)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{k}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{A}_{k+1}\,\mathbf{C}_{k+1}&amp;\mathbf{A}_{k+1}\mathbf{A}_{k+2}\,\mathbf{C}_{k+2}&amp;\cdots&amp;\mathbf{A}_{k+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{T}\,\mathbf{C}_{T}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times(T-k)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.4" class="ltx_p">is the observability map.
The <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,i{-}k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,i{-}k)</annotation></semantics></math> entry of <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{k}\,\mathcal{O}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{k}\,\mathcal{O}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}=T_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}=T_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i{-}k,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i{-}k,j)</annotation></semantics></math> entry of <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{T}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming the transpose relationship. Note that the feedthrough terms <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> do not appear in the past-to-future block (which is strictly below the diagonal), so the factorization depends only on <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> has at most <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> columns and <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> has at most <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> rows,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{rank}\!\bigl(\mathbf{T}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}\bigr)=\operatorname{rank}\!\bigl(\mathcal{R}_{k}\,\mathcal{O}_{k}\bigr)\leq n" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.990em;" width="1.990em"><mi>rank</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:1.990em;" width="1.990em"><mi>rank</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rank}\!\bigl(\mathbf{T}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}\bigr)=\operatorname{rank}\!\bigl(\mathcal{R}_{k}\,\mathcal{O}_{k}\bigr)\leq n</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.5" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence any realization of <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy</p>
<table id="A2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="n\geq\max_{1\leq k&lt;T}\operatorname{rank}\!\bigl(\mathbf{M}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}\bigr)=n_{\min}(\mathbf{X})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></munder><mpadded style="width:1.990em;" width="1.990em"><mi>rank</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq\max_{1\leq k&lt;T}\operatorname{rank}\!\bigl(\mathbf{M}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}\bigr)=n_{\min}(\mathbf{X}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Upper bound.</span> 
Let</p>
<table id="A2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r=n_{\min}(\mathbf{X})=\max_{1\leq k&lt;T}\operatorname{rank}\!\bigl(\mathbf{M}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></munder><mpadded style="width:1.990em;" width="1.990em"><mi>rank</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=n_{\min}(\mathbf{X})=\max_{1\leq k&lt;T}\operatorname{rank}\!\bigl(\mathbf{M}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Because every past-to-future block of <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> has rank at most <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a lower-triangular semiseparable matrix of order <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma B.2 (Finite-Horizon Semiseparable Realization). ‣ B.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix B Realization Theory ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> below, the strictly lower-triangular entries of <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> admit a generator representation</p>
<table id="A2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{ij}=\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i},\qquad i&gt;j," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}=\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i},\qquad i&gt;j,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">with state dimension <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. The diagonal entries are realized independently by choosing</p>
<table id="A2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}=M_{tt}-\mathbf{B}_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}=M_{tt}-\mathbf{B}_{t}\,\mathbf{C}_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.4" class="ltx_p">With these choices, the induced finite horizon input-output matrix <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="A2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ii}=\mathbf{B}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}+D_{i}=\mathbf{B}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}+M_{ii}-\mathbf{B}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}=M_{ii}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ii}=\mathbf{B}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}+D_{i}=\mathbf{B}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}+M_{ii}-\mathbf{B}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}=M_{ii},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.5" class="ltx_p">and, for <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T_{ij}=\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}=M_{ij}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ij}=\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}=M_{ij}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.6" class="ltx_p">Since both <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> are lower triangular, this gives <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}=\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}=\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Thus there exists a finite-horizon linear time-varying realization with state dimension <math id="A2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=n_{\min}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=n_{\min}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the lower-bound argument shows that no realization with smaller state dimension can realize the same finite-horizon input-output map.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.2</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Finite-Horizon Semiseparable Realization)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> be lower triangular and define</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r=\max_{1\leq k&lt;T}\operatorname{rank}\!\bigl(\mathbf{M}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></munder><mpadded style="width:1.990em;" width="1.990em"><mi>rank</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\max_{1\leq k&lt;T}\operatorname{rank}\!\bigl(\mathbf{M}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then the strictly lower-triangular part of <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a linear time-varying realization of state dimension <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> in the transposed convention (i.e. the state if a row vector multiplied by matrices on the right): there exist matrices</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r},\qquad\mathbf{B}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times r},\qquad\mathbf{C}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times 1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r},\qquad\mathbf{B}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times r},\qquad\mathbf{C}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times 1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that, for all <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{ij}=\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}=\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The idea behind the proof is the following. At every time cut <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the past inputs <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},\dots,v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},\dots,v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> influence the future outputs <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k+1},\dots,y_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k+1},\dots,y_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> only through the <em id="A2.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">future output tail</em> <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{k+1}\in\mathbb{R}^{T-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{k+1}\in\mathbb{R}^{T-k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the vector of all future outputs
attributable to those past inputs. The set of all achievable future tails, <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{k}=\operatorname{im}(H_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{k}=\operatorname{im}(H_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, has dimension at most <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> by assumption. We pick an <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional coordinate system <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for this space at each cut, and then express the one-step evolution of the future tail (drop the output just emitted, add the new input’s contribution) as a linear map in those coordinates. This yields the state-space matrices <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for the strictly lower-triangular part directly. The diagonal is handled separately by the feedthrough term <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in the theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each cut <math id="A2.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,T-1</annotation></semantics></math>, define the past-to-future block</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{k}:=\mathbf{M}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}\in\mathbb{R}^{(T-k)\times k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}:=\mathbf{M}_{k+1:T,\;1:k}\in\mathbb{R}^{(T-k)\times k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus <math id="A2.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> maps a past input vector <math id="A2.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{1},\dots,v_{k})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{1},\dots,v_{k})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> to the future output tail <math id="A2.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{k+1},\dots,y_{T})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{k+1},\dots,y_{T})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by those past inputs.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{k}:=\operatorname{im}(H_{k})\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{T-k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{k}:=\operatorname{im}(H_{k})\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{T-k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">By definition, <math id="A2.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\mathcal{X}_{k}=\operatorname{rank}(H_{k})\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\mathcal{X}_{k}=\operatorname{rank}(H_{k})\leq r</annotation></semantics></math>. The space <math id="A2.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reachable future-tail space at time <math id="A2.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>: two past input histories are equivalent if they produce the same future output tail.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each <math id="A2.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, choose a matrix</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{(T-k)\times r}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{(T-k)\times r}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">whose columns form a basis for <math id="A2.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A2.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\mathcal{X}_{k}&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\mathcal{X}_{k}&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math>, extend to a set of <math id="A2.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> columns that still has full column rank (e.g. by appending arbitrary vectors that complete the columns to a linearly independent set in <math id="A2.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{T-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{T-k}</annotation></semantics></math>). In particular, <math id="A2.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> has full column rank <math id="A2.SS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="A2.SS2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}(Q_{k})\supseteq\mathcal{X}_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}(Q_{k})\supseteq\mathcal{X}_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evolution of the future tail.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose the past inputs up to time <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> have produced the future tail</p>
<table id="A2.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{k+1}=H_{k}\begin{bmatrix}v_{1}\\
\vdots\\
v_{k}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathcal{X}_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{k+1}=H_{k}\begin{bmatrix}v_{1}\\
\vdots\\
v_{k}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathcal{X}_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Its first coordinate is the output <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> caused by past inputs; the remaining coordinates are the outputs <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k+2},\dots,y_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k+2},\dots,y_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> caused by those same past inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}:\mathbb{R}^{T-k}\to\mathbb{R}^{T-k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}:\mathbb{R}^{T-k}\to\mathbb{R}^{T-k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> delete the first coordinate. After advancing one step, the old past contribution to the remaining future tail is <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}\,z_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}\,z_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The new input <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> contributes to future outputs <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k+2},\dots,y_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k+2},\dots,y_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> through the column tail</p>
<table id="A2.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="b_{k+1}:=\mathbf{M}_{k+2:T,\;k+1}\in\mathbb{R}^{T-k-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k+1}:=\mathbf{M}_{k+2:T,\;k+1}\in\mathbb{R}^{T-k-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Hence the new future tail is</p>
<table id="A2.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{k+2}=P_{k}\,z_{k+1}+b_{k+1}\,v_{k+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{k+2}=P_{k}\,z_{k+1}+b_{k+1}\,v_{k+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">By construction this vector lies in <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{k+1}=\operatorname{im}(H_{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{k+1}=\operatorname{im}(H_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, because</p>
<table id="A2.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{k+1}\begin{bmatrix}v_{1}\\
\vdots\\
v_{k}\\
v_{k+1}\end{bmatrix}=P_{k}\,H_{k}\begin{bmatrix}v_{1}\\
\vdots\\
v_{k}\end{bmatrix}+\mathbf{M}_{k+2:T,\;k+1}\,v_{k+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k+1}\begin{bmatrix}v_{1}\\
\vdots\\
v_{k}\\
v_{k+1}\end{bmatrix}=P_{k}\,H_{k}\begin{bmatrix}v_{1}\\
\vdots\\
v_{k}\end{bmatrix}+\mathbf{M}_{k+2:T,\;k+1}\,v_{k+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Therefore</p>
<table id="A2.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}(P_{k}\,Q_{k})\subseteq\operatorname{im}(Q_{k+1}),\qquad b_{k+1}\in\operatorname{im}(Q_{k+1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}(P_{k}\,Q_{k})\subseteq\operatorname{im}(Q_{k+1}),\qquad b_{k+1}\in\operatorname{im}(Q_{k+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">State-space matrices.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To express the future-tail recurrence in coordinates, substitute
<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{k+1}=Q_{k}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{k+1}=Q_{k}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{k+2}=Q_{k+1}\,\sigma_{k+2}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{k+2}=Q_{k+1}\,\sigma_{k+2}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{k+2}=P_{k}\,z_{k+1}+b_{k+1}\,v_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{k+2}=P_{k}\,z_{k+1}+b_{k+1}\,v_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, giving</p>
<table id="A2.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k+1}\,\sigma_{k+2}^{\top}=P_{k}\,Q_{k}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}+b_{k+1}\,v_{k+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k+1}\,\sigma_{k+2}^{\top}=P_{k}\,Q_{k}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}+b_{k+1}\,v_{k+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We are looking to find the recurrent relationship between the coordinate representation of the future tails <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k+2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and inputs <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. For this to take the state-space form <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k+2}=\sigma_{k+1}\,\mathbf{A}_{k+1}+v_{k+1}\,\mathbf{B}_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k+2}=\sigma_{k+1}\,\mathbf{A}_{k+1}+v_{k+1}\,\mathbf{B}_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we transpose the desired update to column form,
<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k+2}^{\top}=\mathbf{A}_{k+1}^{\top}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}+\mathbf{B}_{k+1}^{\top}\,v_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k+2}^{\top}=\mathbf{A}_{k+1}^{\top}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}+\mathbf{B}_{k+1}^{\top}\,v_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and substitute into the left-hand side:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k+1}\bigl(\mathbf{A}_{k+1}^{\top}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}+\mathbf{B}_{k+1}^{\top}\,v_{k+1}\bigr)=P_{k}\,Q_{k}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}+b_{k+1}\,v_{k+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k+1}\bigl(\mathbf{A}_{k+1}^{\top}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}+\mathbf{B}_{k+1}^{\top}\,v_{k+1}\bigr)=P_{k}\,Q_{k}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}+b_{k+1}\,v_{k+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Expanding and matching the coefficients of <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> independently (since the identity must hold for every input history and every new input), we obtain</p>
<table id="A2.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k+1}\,\mathbf{A}_{k+1}^{\top}=P_{k}\,Q_{k},\qquad Q_{k+1}\,\mathbf{B}_{k+1}^{\top}=b_{k+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k+1}\,\mathbf{A}_{k+1}^{\top}=P_{k}\,Q_{k},\qquad Q_{k+1}\,\mathbf{B}_{k+1}^{\top}=b_{k+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The first equation says that <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{k+1}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{k+1}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is the change of basis from the old coordinate system <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to the new one <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> after the first output has been emitted (i.e. after applying <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>). The second equation says that <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{k+1}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{k+1}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes the new input’s contribution in the new coordinates.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> has full column rank <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> (and hence admits a left inverse), both equations have unique solutions:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{k+1}^{\top}=Q_{k+1}^{+}\,P_{k}\,Q_{k},\qquad\mathbf{B}_{k+1}^{\top}=Q_{k+1}^{+}\,b_{k+1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{k+1}^{\top}=Q_{k+1}^{+}\,P_{k}\,Q_{k},\qquad\mathbf{B}_{k+1}^{\top}=Q_{k+1}^{+}\,b_{k+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k+1}^{+}=(Q_{k+1}^{\top}Q_{k+1})^{-1}Q_{k+1}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k+1}^{+}=(Q_{k+1}^{\top}Q_{k+1})^{-1}Q_{k+1}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is the left inverse.
Transposing gives <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the row-vector convention directly.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The output at time <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> caused by the past is the first coordinate of <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{k+1}=Q_{k}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{k+1}=Q_{k}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, this coordinate is</p>
<table id="A2.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k+1}=e_{1}^{\top}\,z_{k+1}=e_{1}^{\top}\,Q_{k}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}=\sigma_{k+1}\,Q_{k}^{\top}\,e_{1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k+1}=e_{1}^{\top}\,z_{k+1}=e_{1}^{\top}\,Q_{k}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}=\sigma_{k+1}\,Q_{k}^{\top}\,e_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the last step transposes a scalar. Comparing with the desired output equation
<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k+1}=\sigma_{k+1}\,\mathbf{C}_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k+1}=\sigma_{k+1}\,\mathbf{C}_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</p>
<table id="A2.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{k+1}:=Q_{k}^{\top}\,e_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times 1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{k+1}:=Q_{k}^{\top}\,e_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times 1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the first standard basis vector in <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{T-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{T-k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Coordinate state.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k+1}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k+1}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> (a row vector) satisfy
<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{k+1}=Q_{k}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{k+1}=Q_{k}\,\sigma_{k+1}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k+1}=(Q_{k}^{+}\,z_{k+1})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k+1}=(Q_{k}^{+}\,z_{k+1})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The dynamics become</p>
<table id="A2.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k+2}=\sigma_{k+1}\,\mathbf{A}_{k+1}+v_{k+1}\,\mathbf{B}_{k+1},\qquad y_{k+1}^{\text{past}}=\sigma_{k+1}\,\mathbf{C}_{k+1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mtext>past</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k+2}=\sigma_{k+1}\,\mathbf{A}_{k+1}+v_{k+1}\,\mathbf{B}_{k+1},\qquad y_{k+1}^{\text{past}}=\sigma_{k+1}\,\mathbf{C}_{k+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k+1}^{\text{past}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mtext>past</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k+1}^{\text{past}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the contribution of past inputs <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},\dots,v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},\dots,v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to the output at time <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>. The contribution of the current input <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is not captured by the future-tail construction; it is handled by the feedthrough term <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the theorem.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Verification.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Chaining the one-step updates, the contribution of input <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to output
<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="A2.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v_{j}\;\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}=v_{j}\;M_{ij}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j}\;\mathbf{B}_{j}\,\mathbf{A}_{j+1}\cdots\mathbf{A}_{i}\,\mathbf{C}_{i}=v_{j}\;M_{ij}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Hence <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ij}=M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ij}=M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ij}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ij}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> by causality. The diagonal entries are not determined by this construction; they are
realized by the feedthrough term <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in the theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Thus the strictly lower-triangular part of <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a linear time-varying realization of state dimension <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> in the transposed convention.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark B.3</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>(Choice of basis <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof requires only that <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> has full column rank <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and spans <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{k}=\operatorname{im}(H_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{k}=\operatorname{im}(H_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>; different choices yield different realizations <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{B}_{t},\mathbf{C}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{A}_{t},\mathbf{B}_{t},\mathbf{C}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> related by time-varying similarity transforms, all producing the same finite horizon input output matrix <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Several concrete choices are natural:</p>
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SVD basis.</span>
Take <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> from the thin SVD of <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}=U_{k}\Sigma_{k}V_{k}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}=U_{k}\Sigma_{k}V_{k}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>: use the first <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}=\operatorname{rank}(H_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}=\operatorname{rank}(H_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> left singular vectors as columns and pad with <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> orthonormal vectors from the orthogonal complement. The resulting <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> has orthonormal columns, so the left inverse reduces to <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}^{+}=Q_{k}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}^{+}=Q_{k}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, giving <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{k+1}^{\top}=Q_{k+1}^{\top}P_{k}\,Q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{k+1}^{\top}=Q_{k+1}^{\top}P_{k}\,Q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{k+1}^{\top}=Q_{k+1}^{\top}b_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{k+1}^{\top}=Q_{k+1}^{\top}b_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Balanced realization.</span>
Choose <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> so that the reachability and observability Gramians of the resulting realization are equal and diagonal. Balanced realizations are the basis of balanced truncation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gugercin and Antoulas, 2004</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sandberg and Rantzer, 2004</a>]</cite>, which provides near-optimal model reduction with <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm error bounds for time-invariant systems.
For the time-varying finite-horizon setting considered here, balanced truncation remains applicable but the optimality guarantees are weaker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sandberg and Rantzer, 2004</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">When the goal is exact realization (state dimension equals <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>), all choices are equivalent up to similarity. The distinction matters when the state dimension is reduced below <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> (approximate realization): the truncated SVD basis minimizes the per-cut Frobenius error for an individual Hankel block, whereas balanced truncation uses reachability and observability information to produce a system-aware approximation of the entire input-output map.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Layer Selection Procedure</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix C Layer Selection Procedure ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> details the importance scoring procedure used to select which Attention layers to replace with SSM layers during Priming. The procedure is training-free: for each layer, we substitute its full-context Attention with Sliding Window Attention (SWA) and measure the resulting degradation on a suite of long-context benchmarks. Layers that tolerate SWA well are safe candidates for SSM instantiation, while layers that degrade significantly are kept as Attention. As discussed in <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" title="Importance-based layer selection algorithm. ‣ 3.2.1 Layer Selection: Avoiding the Hardest Cases ‣ 3.2 The Priming Pipeline as Approximation of the Realization-Theoretic Ideal ‣ 3 What Is Priming and Why Does It Work? ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>, SWA serves as an empirical proxy for the effective Hankel rank of each layer’s mixing matrix, layers with high Hankel rank rely on long-range structure that a fixed-size recurrent state cannot compress.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Layer Selection via Importance Scoring</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span>Source Transformer <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> Attention layers; number of SSM layers <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>; SWA window size <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>; evaluation benchmark suite <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> </span>Set of layers <math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{convert}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>convert</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{convert}}</annotation></semantics></math> to instantiate as SSM layers
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">each Attention layer <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0,\ldots,L{-}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0,\ldots,L{-}1</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Create variant <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>: replace only layer <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> with SWA (window size <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>);
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Evaluate <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain scores <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> for each task <math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline">Compute mean retained performance per layer: <math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}^{(i)}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}|}\sum_{t}s_{t}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}^{(i)}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}|}\sum_{t}s_{t}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline">Rank layers by <math id="algorithm1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> in descending order (highest = least degradation);
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{convert}}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>convert</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{convert}}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> top-<math id="algorithm1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> layers by rank;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.9.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{convert}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>convert</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{convert}}</annotation></semantics></math></em>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Additional Reasoning Results</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>SciCode Problem vs. Subtask Accuracy</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SciCode <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2024</a>]</cite> decomposes each research-coding problem into several <em id="A4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">subtasks</em>: a solution is counted as correct at the problem level only if <em id="A4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">every</em> subtask is solved, making problem-level accuracy much lower than subtask-level accuracy and highly sensitive to a single failing step. In the main paper (<a href="#S5.T4" title="In 5.2 Primed Reasoning Model Results ‣ 5 Priming: Main Results ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tables</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.2 Primed Reasoning Model Results ‣ 5 Priming: Main Results ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) we report problem-level pass @1, which often yields single-digit absolute numbers for models at 8B. Many publicly reported SciCode numbers instead use subtask-level pass @1, which runs roughly three to four times higher in absolute terms.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#A4.T18" title="In D.1 SciCode Problem vs. Subtask Accuracy ‣ Appendix D Additional Reasoning Results ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> we report both metrics side by side for the models in our reasoning evaluation suite. Relative rankings are identical
under the two metrics. At 8B: Qwen3-8B <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> GKA-Primed <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> Qwen3-8B [Reasoner-SFT] <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> GDN-Primed, on both problem and subtask accuracy. At 32B: Qwen3-32B <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> Qwen3-32B [Reasoner-SFT] <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> GKA-Primed, again on both metrics. The qualitative conclusions of the main paper (both on ranking and on post-training regressions relative to the off-the-shelf checkpoint) are therefore unaffected by the choice of metric.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T18" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A4.T18.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 18</span>: </span><span id="A4.T18.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">SciCode problem vs. subtask accuracy.<span id="A4.T18.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Problem-level numbers
reproduce <a href="#S5.T4" title="In 5.2 Primed Reasoning Model Results ‣ 5 Priming: Main Results ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tables</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.2 Primed Reasoning Model Results ‣ 5 Priming: Main Results ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> from the main paper.
Subtask-level numbers are the standard metric reported in the
SciCode <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2024</a>]</cite> paper and by third parties. Relative rankings
are preserved under both metrics.</span></span></figcaption>
