<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Choosing Inference Parameters for Test-Time Scaling</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our work we use vLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2023</a>]</cite> to scale inference deployment of our models. vLLM serves requests using continuous batching: the scheduler maintains waiting and running sets of requests and, at each engine step, selects a batch of prefill and/or decode work subject to token and KV-cache constraints. The running set is bounded by the KV-cache budget: when GPU memory reserved for KV caches is fully committed, the engine is <em id="A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">KV-cache saturated</em> and no new requests can be admitted until a running one completes. If the running set later exceeds what the budget can sustain (e.g., admitted sequences grew longer than anticipated at admission time), the scheduler <em id="A4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">preempts</em> some requests and returns them to the waiting queue; when a preempted request is re-admitted, much of its prefill must be re-run, which results in some throughput overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our test-time scaling experiment (<a href="#S6" title="6 Test-Time Scaling: Intelligence per FLOP ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) triggers exactly this preemption regime: AIME 2025 rollouts mix easy and hard problems, so short correct traces finish early while long traces from hard problems accumulate and saturate the KV budget, forcing the scheduler to preempt the remaining running requests. One way to limit this is to cap how many sequences the scheduler may admit concurrently, a lower cap trades early-run throughput on short-trace rollouts for less late-run preemption once long traces pile up. The vLLM argument <span id="A4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max_num_seqs</span> is exactly this cap on the running queue. The right setting is architecture-dependent: the Hybrid’s smaller per-sequence KV footprint supports more concurrent sequences before saturation than the Transformer’s, so their optima differ. We sweep it per model and report the fastest configuration.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Protocol.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each (model, <span id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max_num_seqs</span>) pair we run the full AIME 2025 set (30 problems) at five rollout counts <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{b}\in\{480,720,960,1200,1440\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>480,720,960</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1200</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1440</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{b}\in\{480,720,960,1200,1440\}</annotation></semantics></math> on 8<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>H200 (TP<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=</annotation></semantics></math>8) with identical sampling parameters. From the per-request server logs we take each run’s wall-clock as <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{wc}_{b}=\max(\text{end})-\min(\text{start})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>wc</mtext><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>end</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>start</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{wc}_{b}=\max(\text{end})-\min(\text{start})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., end-to-end elapsed time from first dispatch to last completion. We summarize each pair with a rollout-weighted mean, <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sec/rollout}=\sum_{b}\text{wc}_{b}\,/\,\sum_{b}n_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sec/rollout</mtext><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>b</mi></msub><msub><mtext>wc</mtext><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>b</mi></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sec/rollout}=\sum_{b}\text{wc}_{b}\,/\,\sum_{b}n_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> indexes the five rollout counts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Observations.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GKA-Primed-HQwen3-Reasoner’s throughput degrades monotonically above <span id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max_num_seqs<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{=}256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{=}256</annotation></semantics></math></span> (4.6<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>5.0<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>5.3 sec/rollout from 256 to 768): once the ceiling exceeds the KV-saturation concurrency, additional admissions trigger preemption, and the re-prefill cost dominates any batching gain. The Qwen3-32B [Reasoner-SFT] baseline is comparatively insensitive to this knob over the same range (5.9<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>5.8 sec/rollout from 256 to 1024). At their respective optima (GKA-Primed: 256; Qwen3-32B [Reasoner-SFT]: 512), GKA-Primed-HQwen3-Reasoner serves AIME 2025 at 21% lower sec/rollout (4.6 vs. 5.8) at comparable accuracy. Dropping Chebyshev iterations to <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}10</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S8.SS2" title="8.2 Variable Test-time Compute with GKA ‣ 8 Gated KalmaNet: A Fading Memory SSM Layer with Variable Test-time Compute ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.2</span></a>) at the same <span id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max_num_seqs<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{=}256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{=}256</annotation></semantics></math></span> reduces sec/rollout further to 4.3 with a <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>1% accuracy cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Hybrid State Composition Theoretical Grounding</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each SSM family, the recurrence structure admits a principled decomposition that justifies combining independently computed states (at least in the single layer setting). We describe the CASO/PICASO argument for Mamba2 (where it was originally derived), show how it extends to GDN and GKA with different computational tradeoffs, and then show that GKA additionally admits a simpler additive composition in the ungated limit via the information filter.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mamba2: CASO and PICASO.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 SSM Layer Types ‣ 4 SSM Layers for Fading Memory in Hybrids ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> that Mamba2 updates its state as
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}=\alpha_{t}\,\mathbf{S}_{t-1}+\mathbf{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}=\alpha_{t}\,\mathbf{S}_{t-1}+\mathbf{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a single-layer SSM, the state obtained by processing the concatenation of <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> chunks <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1},\ldots,u_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1},\ldots,u_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed exactly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="A5.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}(u_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot u_{K})=\mathbf{S}^{(K)}+\sum_{c=1}^{K-1}\Bigl(\prod_{j=c+1}^{K}\mathbf{A}^{(j)}\Bigr)\,\mathbf{S}^{(c)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}(u_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot u_{K})=\mathbf{S}^{(K)}+\sum_{c=1}^{K-1}\Bigl(\prod_{j=c+1}^{K}\mathbf{A}^{(j)}\Bigr)\,\mathbf{S}^{(c)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}^{(c)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}^{(c)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the state from chunk <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> alone and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}^{(c)}=\prod_{t\in\text{chunk}\,c}\mathrm{diag}(\alpha_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>chunk</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}^{(c)}=\prod_{t\in\text{chunk}\,c}\mathrm{diag}(\alpha_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the accumulated decay matrix for that chunk. Because Mamba2 uses diagonal transitions, <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}^{(c)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}^{(c)}</annotation></semantics></math> is diagonal and the products reduce to element-wise arithmetic—no forward passes are needed.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This is the CASO (Compositional Aggregation of States as Observations) formula. It is exact for a single layer but order-dependent: the result changes with the permutation of chunks. PICASO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>]</cite> removes this dependence by averaging over permutations. The cyclic variant (PICASO-R) averages over <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> cyclic shifts and is computable in <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(K)</annotation></semantics></math> time, and is what we implement as part of state composition for this layer.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">GDN: PICASO with dense transition matrices.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GDN’s recurrence (<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 SSM Layer Types ‣ 4 SSM Layers for Fading Memory in Hybrids ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) is
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}=\alpha_{t}(\mathbf{I}-\beta_{t}\,\mathbf{k}_{t}\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top})\,\mathbf{S}_{t-1}+\beta_{t}\,\mathbf{v}_{t}\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}=\alpha_{t}(\mathbf{I}-\beta_{t}\,\mathbf{k}_{t}\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top})\,\mathbf{S}_{t-1}+\beta_{t}\,\mathbf{v}_{t}\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This has the same <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{A}_{t}\,\mathbf{S}_{t-1}+\mathbf{B}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{A}_{t}\,\mathbf{S}_{t-1}+\mathbf{B}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> structure as Mamba2, so the CASO decomposition
(Eq. <a href="#A5.E29" title="Equation 29 ‣ Mamba2: CASO and PICASO. ‣ Appendix E Hybrid State Composition Theoretical Grounding ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) applies directly with PICASO averaging restoring approximate permutation invariance. The key difference is computational: Mamba2’s diagonal <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> yields a diagonal accumulated product <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}^{(c)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}^{(c)}</annotation></semantics></math>, so CASO reduces to element-wise operations in <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>. For GDN, each per-step transition <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}=\alpha_{t}(\mathbf{I}-\beta_{t}\,\mathbf{k}_{t}\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}=\alpha_{t}(\mathbf{I}-\beta_{t}\,\mathbf{k}_{t}\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math> is a rank-1 perturbation of a scaled identity, and their product <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}^{(c)}=\prod_{t\in\text{chunk}\,c}\mathbf{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>chunk</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}^{(c)}=\prod_{t\in\text{chunk}\,c}\mathbf{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a product of Householder-like matrices. In our current implementation we materialize the accumulated product <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}^{(c)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}^{(c)}</annotation></semantics></math> as a dense <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}\times d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}\times d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> matrix; however, the rank-1 structure of each factor could be exploited for a more compact representation, which we leave to future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">GKA: additive composition via the information filter.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GKA maintains a gated information matrix <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and information state <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as its internal states (see <a href="#S8" title="8 Gated KalmaNet: A Fading Memory SSM Layer with Variable Test-time Compute ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> for the full parameterization, including the adaptive regularization <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="A5.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{H}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{H}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\gamma_{t}\,\mathbf{H}_{t-1}+\beta_{t}\mathbf{k}_{t}\,\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\gamma_{t}\,\mathbf{H}_{t-1}+\beta_{t}\mathbf{k}_{t}\,\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{U}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{U}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\gamma_{t}\,\mathbf{U}_{t-1}+\beta_{t}\mathbf{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\gamma_{t}\,\mathbf{U}_{t-1}+\beta_{t}\mathbf{v}_{t}\,\mathbf{k}_{t}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned decay gate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>]</cite>. Note that while the output state is a non-linear combination of these terms,
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{U}_{t}\,(\mathbf{H}_{t}+\lambda_{t}\mathbf{I})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{t}=\mathbf{U}_{t}\,(\mathbf{H}_{t}+\lambda_{t}\mathbf{I})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the state updates themselves are affine.
Since <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{t}=\gamma_{t}\,\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{t}=\gamma_{t}\,\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is diagonal (a scalar times identity), the CASO decomposition (Eq. <a href="#A5.E29" title="Equation 29 ‣ Mamba2: CASO and PICASO. ‣ Appendix E Hybrid State Composition Theoretical Grounding ‣ Priming: Hybrid State Space Models From Pre-trained Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) applies with the same element-wise efficiency as Mamba2, and PICASO averaging restores approximate permutation invariance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, GKA also admits a simpler composition. In the ungated limit (<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}=1</annotation></semantics></math>), both <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> accumulate purely additively, so states from independently processed chunks compose by summation:</p>
<table id="A5.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\mathrm{merged}}=\sum_{c=1}^{K}\mathbf{H}^{(c)},\qquad\mathbf{U}_{\mathrm{merged}}=\sum_{c=1}^{K}\mathbf{U}^{(c)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>merged</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>merged</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\mathrm{merged}}=\sum_{c=1}^{K}\mathbf{H}^{(c)},\qquad\mathbf{U}_{\mathrm{merged}}=\sum_{c=1}^{K}\mathbf{U}^{(c)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This is the multi-sensor fusion identity from classical Kalman filtering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mutambara, 1998</a>]</cite>: if <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> sensors observe the same latent state with shared dynamics, their information matrices and states can be summed to obtain the fused posterior. When <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the additive composition is no longer exact; however, for sufficiently long chunks the gated decay suppresses the contribution of early tokens, making the additive approximation effective in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Symmetric Tiled Triton Kernel: Implementation Details</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Updating the Information Matrix <math id="A6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (State-update loop).</span> Let <math id="A6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="A6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>-th tile. We iterate over lower-triangular tiles (<math id="A6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq j</annotation></semantics></math>), and for each:</p>
<ol id="A6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Load <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{t-1}[i,j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{t-1}[i,j]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{t}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{t}[i]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{t}[j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{t}[j]</annotation></semantics></math> from HBM.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compute <math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j]\leftarrow\gamma_{t}\mathbf{H}_{t-1}[i,j]+\beta_{t}\mathbf{k}_{t}[i]\mathbf{k}_{t}[j]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j]\leftarrow\gamma_{t}\mathbf{H}_{t-1}[i,j]+\beta_{t}\mathbf{k}_{t}[i]\mathbf{k}_{t}[j]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Accumulate the tile’s contribution to <math id="A6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{H}_{t}\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{H}_{t}\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, doubling the contribution for off-diagonal tiles (<math id="A6.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math>) to account for the mirrored upper-triangular half.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Store <math id="A6.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j]</annotation></semantics></math> back to HBM.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A final square root over the accumulator gives <math id="A6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{H}_{t}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{H}_{t}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, which sets the regularization strength <math id="A6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{t}=\alpha\|\mathbf{H}_{t}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{t}=\alpha\|\mathbf{H}_{t}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> used by the CH. The Information State <math id="A6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is updated with the same tile-streaming pattern, but since it is not symmetric every tile is read, updated, and written back.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CH iterations.</span> Each CH iteration requires the product <math id="A6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{t}^{(k)}=\mathbf{H}_{t}\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(k-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{t}^{(k)}=\mathbf{H}_{t}\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(k-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(k-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(k-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the CH solution obtained after <math id="A6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)</annotation></semantics></math> iterations. This computation can be decomposed over tiles. Let <math id="A6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(k-1)}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(k-1)}[i]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="A6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th tile of <math id="A6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(k-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(k-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{t}^{(k)}[j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{t}^{(k)}[j]</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="A6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th tile of the output. We loop over all tile pairs <math id="A6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="A6.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A6.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq j</annotation></semantics></math>, reload <math id="A6.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j]</annotation></semantics></math> from HBM, for <span id="A6.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tiled_large_batch</span> variant. For <span id="A6.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tiled_small_batch</span> variant, just use previously-computed <math id="A6.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j]</annotation></semantics></math> tile which is already in registers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A6.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, recompute <math id="A6.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j]=\mathbf{H}_{t}[j,i]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j]=\mathbf{H}_{t}[j,i]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> on the fly from its lower-triangular mirror; we do not persist this tile to HBM, as re-transposing in each CH iteration is cheaper on GPUs (like H200s) than an HBM write-back and reload.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Accumulate <math id="A6.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{t}^{(k)}[i]\mathrel{+}=\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j],\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(k-1)}[j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.056em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">+</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{t}^{(k)}[i]\mathrel{+}=\mathbf{H}_{t}[i,j],\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(k-1)}[j]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Once every tile pair has been processed, <math id="A6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{t}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{t}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> is complete. The remaining per-iteration CH update <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>, Algorithm 1]</cite> involves only pointwise operations on the full vectors, which do not benefit from tiling, so we join the tiles into contiguous vectors, perform the update, and split the resulting <math id="A6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> back into tiles for the next iteration. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
